    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 413,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Enforce the 8-char password server-side (`auths.py`) \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (200\u2192bug, 400\u2192fixed); committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 414,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Fix `UserUpdateForm` / `SpaceForm` TS\u2194Pydantic drift \u2014 committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 415,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Delete the dead `SUPPORTED_FILE_EXTENSIONS` copy in `constants.ts` (backend 50-entry list is live) \u2014 committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 416,
          "tags": [
            "#security",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Reconcile the drifted permission-tree defaults** (2026-07-27) \u2014 the backend described user permissions in TWO independent places and nothing kept them in agreement: the env-derived `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS` table (`config.py`, what `has_permission()` ENFORCES) and hardcoded Pydantic model defaults (`routers/users.py`, what the admin panel ADVERTISES). Diffed them exactly by importing both inside the image: **4 value disagreements** (`sharing.public_models/public_knowledge/public_prompts/public_tools` \u2014 env `False`, Pydantic `True`) and **1 key missing entirely** (`features.web_search` existed only in Pydantic). The 4 sharing ones are LATENT \u2014 masked because the persisted `USER_PERMISSIONS` carries those keys, so stored values override the Pydantic defaults \u2014 but they are a loaded gun for any future key. `features.web_search` was live and user-visible: the panel advertised it on, enforcement read the absent key as denied, and `MessageInput.svelte:469-470` gates the web-search button on `$user.permissions.features.web_search`, so **no non-admin ever got the button while the panel said they had it**. Fixes: (a) added `USER_PERMISSIONS_FEATURES_WEB_SEARCH` (default `True`, matching what the panel always advertised and the sibling features; exposure stays gated by the global `enable_web_search` flag, so this switches nothing on for deployments that have not enabled web search) and wired it into the table; (b) the Pydantic model defaults now DERIVE from `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS` instead of restating it, so the panel cannot advertise a policy the backend does not apply \u2014 and a Pydantic field with no table entry now raises `KeyError` at import, i.e. the container fails loudly at boot instead of shipping a silent policy lie; (c) `get_permissions()` (`utils/access_control.py`) now backfills from `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS` as well as from the saved config \u2014 without this the fix would never reach an UPGRADED deployment, whose stored dict predates the key, leaving `has_permission()` allowing it while the payload the frontend renders omitted it. Guard-rail `permissions-drift.cy.ts` asserts the observable contract rather than the internal tables: every advertised key must be enforced, values must match, and `features.web_search` is pinned by name. Measure-twice: RED pre-change (2 of 3 failing, `features.web_search advertised but not enforced`), GREEN after. Deliberately ONE-directional \u2014 a v1.1.1 database still carries a retired `workspace` group from before this fork renamed it `workshop`, so asserting equality would fail on every upgraded deployment for no reason. **Verified on real legacy data**: booted this image on a copy of the v1.1.1 prod snapshot via `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` (20/21, see below) and compared both endpoints directly \u2014 `features` identical on both sides including `web_search: true`. That legacy check was done by direct API comparison, not through the Cypress spec, because the Cypress container and the gate's container are not on a shared docker network. Frontend note: all 13 `?? true`/`?? false` permission defaults in `src/` already MATCH the backend (checked mechanically, 86 permission reads total) \u2014 no frontend change needed, though those fallbacks are now dead weight that could mask future drift, filed below. Gates: `make e2e` 13/15, only the two known pre-existing leaks failing. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 417,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "**Drop the frontend's permission fallbacks** (follow-up to the reconcile above): the 13 `$user?.permissions?.x?.y ?? true` sites in `src/` all match the backend today, and now that every enforced key is guaranteed present in the payload they are unreachable defaults. Worse, they can only ever mask future drift \u2014 a `?? true` renders a control the API will refuse. Delete them so the frontend reads what the backend sent, with a guard-rail that a denied permission hides its control."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 418,
          "tags": [
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**RESOLVED \u2014 it was never a product bug: `| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`** (2026-07-28). The check greps the target-arch container's logs for `Sprig\u2122 host architecture: amd64`, and it was recorded as either a normalization bug or an emulation quirk. It was neither. **`writer | grep -q PATTERN` under pipefail reports a MATCH as a FAILURE**: grep exits on the first hit, the writer still has output queued, takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, and pipefail surfaces 141 as the pipeline's status. It only fires when the writer had more to say, which is why it looked like flakiness \u2014 the same assertion passes on a quiet container and fails on a busy one. Reproduced deterministically (200k-line writer \u2192 status 141 on a match, five runs out of five). **The same bug cost a second gate**: `sprig_signing`'s \"minisign OK\" check failed inside `gauntlet_full` and passed in isolation, and I nearly filed it as a flake. **And it had a worse third form nobody had noticed**: `upgrade-gate.sh:55` asserts the ABSENCE of a traceback in the migration logs \u2014 there, 141 reads as \"no match\", so the gate reported clean on exactly the failure it exists to catch. That check could not have caught a migration traceback on production data. Devices, in preference order: (1) pipe-free primitives in `scripts/lib/gate.sh` \u2014 `text_has`, `log_has`, `log_has_i`, `wait_for_log`, `fetch_has` \u2014 which capture the text and match with bash's own operators, so there is no writer to kill and no status to misread; (2) every unbounded-writer site converted (2 `docker logs` in the gates, 1 in `wizard-smoke.sh`, 8 `curl` in the two smoke gates, 1 in `build-sprig-tika.sh`); (3) `make pipefail_lint` refuses the shape returning, verified to FAIL on a reintroduced line and pass once removed; (4) `make pipefail_fixture` proves BOTH halves \u2014 that the trap is real AND that the helpers fix it \u2014 because a device that fixes nothing looks identical to one that works unless the broken shape is asserted too. Both wired into `gauntlet_full`; the lint also gates `make lint`. Scope is deliberate and written into the lint: only `docker logs` and `curl` writers are banned, because `printf`/`echo` are builtins on in-memory strings that finish before grep can exit \u2014 widen it if a `printf` haystack ever passes 64 KB. Verified after: `gauntlet_full` exit 0 (fixture 7/7, smoke 66/66, durability 12/12, signing 10/10, ui-Sprig 22/22, parity green, e2e 46 passing), `upgrade_gate` **21/21**."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 419,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Cruft sweep (deps): removed unused `chart.js` + `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` from package.json (zero code refs \u2014 grep + a clean vite build both confirm) and the dead `DOMPurify` import in `MarkdownInlineTokens.svelte`. bun.lock reconciled (`Removed: 2`); `it_build` frozen-install + build green; markdown/sanitizer guard-rails green. (A fuller depcheck of the other 100+ deps is a separate pass.) Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 420,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Strip hyperscript attribute forms (`_`, `script`, `data-script`) in the user-content sanitizer \u2014 one app-wide DOMPurify `uponSanitizeAttribute` hook (`utils/sanitize.ts`, installed in root `+layout.svelte`) covers all 14 `@html` sites; `data-script` survived DOMPurify's default `ALLOW_DATA_ATTR`, the other two were already dropped. Defense-in-depth (hyperscript not yet loaded). Guard-rail `content-security-sanitizer.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-fix (data-script leaked via a rendered banner), GREEN post. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 421,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: `Cache-Control: immutable` on `_app/immutable/*` \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (empty header\u2192immutable); committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 422,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: eager ML libs off the conversation chunk (nodes/2) \u2014 MEASURED: the 22 MB WASM was already lazy (feature-gated, NOT on the load path; the audit inferred wrong). Real win: deleted dead `@huggingface/transformers` import + made kokoro-js dynamic in `chat/Settings/Audio.svelte`. Fresh-build verified: nodes/2 2.70\u21920.65 MB, app load 5.39\u21924.12 MB transfer (\u221224%), 13.4\u219210.6 MB decoded; auth+cache guard-rails green. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 423,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: `/icons/` waste \u2014 RESOLVED as already-gated. Image ships 0 `.xcf` (`.dockerignore **/*.xcf`) and 1.3 MB icons total; the audit read the stale host build. The 6 identical 170 KB PNGs are placeholder art meant to diverge (favicon/logo/splash + dark variants) \u2192 branding-art task, not perf."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 424,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "Perf/arch: lazy-load markdown-render libs (katex/mermaid/highlight/codemirror) \u2014 SHIPPED as architecture hardening, MEASURED as perf-neutral. Four changes landed: (A) deleted the DEAD `import katex` in `utils/marked/katex-extension.ts` (tokenizer never used it; real render is `KatexRenderer.svelte`, untouched \u2192 tex still renders); (B) `hljs` now dynamic-imported inside `copyToClipboard` (copy-only, off every page's utils chunk); (C+D) `CodeBlock` + `KatexRenderer` load via memoized `{#await import()}` loaders (`Markdown/lazy.ts`) only when a code/math token renders \u2014 streaming-safe (one promise identity, no re-pending flash). MEASURE-TWICE FINDING: the hoped-for \"plain convo sheds ~10 MB\" is NOT real \u2014 mermaid/codemirror/katex were ALREADY lazy (Vite component chunking); a plain convo never pulled them, before or after (measured: release-3.0.0 vs develop, identical 13.41\u219210.27 delta = the nodes/2 win alone; no separate mermaid/codemirror chunk fetched in EITHER build). So this change's value is real but not bytes: dead-code removal, hljs deferral, and EXPLICIT/structural laziness aligned with the plan's islands principle (robust vs a future refactor making them eager). Guard-rail `markdown-render-lazy.cy.ts` GREEN before AND after (seeds a chat, asserts `.katex` + `.copy-code-button` + `pre.mermaid` render; plain-text control asserts none) \u2014 proves the refactor broke no rendering."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 425,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: batch the serial boot waterfall into `Promise.all` \u2014 the `(app)/+layout.svelte` boot ladder awaited `getModels` \u2192 `getBanners` \u2192 `getTools` \u2192 `getToolServersData` one at a time, though only the first depends on anything (user settings, for `directConnections`). Now one wave after settings. MEASURED (fresh container, `boot-waterfall-timing.json`): BEFORE the three were strictly disjoint \u2014 models `272.7\u21921041.3`, banners `1043.9\u21921050.2`, tools `1052.8\u21921060.3`; AFTER all three start within 1 ms of each other and banners/tools finish well inside the models window. Local saving is small in absolute terms (~19 ms, since banners/tools are ~7 ms each on loopback) but it is three round-trips removed, so the win scales with RTT \u2014 on a 60 ms link that is ~180 ms off first paint. Guard-rail `boot-waterfall.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-change (last start 1153.1 ms vs first end 1139.7 ms \u2014 no overlap), GREEN after. The spec also pins the dependency that must SURVIVE: settings still resolves before `/api/models` is issued. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 426,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**Perf: caching pass** (2026-07-26) \u2014 surveyed every layer that could take a cache, then fixed the two that paid. Framing first, because it decides everything else: the \"$5 VPS serves 100k concurrent\" pattern comes from caching *anonymous identical HTML*, and this app has almost none \u2014 nearly every response is per-user authenticated, plus streaming chat over websockets. **(1) Base-models cache, the big one.** `/api/models` probes every configured provider on every call. MEASURED live against the shipped image: uncached **~740 ms**, cached **~4.5 ms** (~160\u00d7), and `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaulted to `False`. Flipped the default to `True` (`config.py`), which also engages the existing startup warm (`main.py:732`) so even the first request is cheap. The reason it had been off is that the cache has NO TTL and nothing cleared it \u2014 so added `invalidate_base_models_cache()` (`utils/models.py`) and called it from both provider-config update endpoints (`routers/openai.py`, `routers/ollama.py`; imported at call site because `utils.models` imports both routers and a top-level import closes the cycle). Custom models need no invalidation \u2014 they merge fresh on top of the cached base list every call. Guard-rail `models-cache.cy.ts` asserts BOTH halves, because a fast cache with broken invalidation is worse than the slow path: median `/api/models` under 250 ms, AND a provider-config write forces a re-probe, AND the cache refills after. Measure-twice: RED pre-change (median **749 ms**), GREEN after. **(2) `/static` and `/assets` shipped no `Cache-Control`** \u2014 verified live with curl: ETag only, so every repeat visit spends a revalidation round-trip per asset. Added `CachedStaticFiles` (`main.py`) with a BOUNDED `public, max-age=604800`, deliberately not `immutable`: these filenames are reused across deploys (STATIC_DIR re-syncs from the build at startup), so a week is the worst-case staleness. Theme CSS is untouched \u2014 it has its own route with an explicit `no-cache`. `static-caching.cy.ts` grew two tests (bounded-but-not-immutable on `/static`, and the SPA shell must NOT carry a long max-age); RED pre-change on the new one. **COMBINED RESULT, same spec, same harness: boot data-fetch span 821.5 ms \u2192 102.5 ms (8\u00d7), the fetch wave itself 787.6 ms \u2192 21.6 ms, `/api/models` 768 ms \u2192 17 ms.** Gates: `make e2e` 12/14, only the two known pre-existing leaks failing. Caveat for upgrades: `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` is a `PersistentConfig`, so a deployment where an admin explicitly saved this setting keeps their saved choice; everyone else picks up the new default. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 427,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "**`@cached(ttl=MODELS_CACHE_TTL)` never hits \u2014 the decorator is inert** (found during the caching pass, 2026-07-26): `routers/openai.py:550` and `routers/ollama.py:378` use bare `aiocache.cached` with no `key_builder`. aiocache's default key is built from `str(args)`, and the first arg is a Starlette `Request` \u2014 a new object with a new address on every call \u2014 so the key never repeats. PROVEN: with `MODELS_CACHE_TTL=60`, five back-to-back `/api/models` calls took 0.74 / 0.73 / 0.78 / 1.70 / 0.74 s. Zero hits. Two consequences: `MODELS_CACHE_TTL` is a knob that does nothing, and every request stores an entry held for the TTL, so memory grows with request rate. Fix is a `key_builder` keyed on stable values (user id + provider-config revision) \u2014 or delete the decorators, since removing something that never hits is behaviour-preserving. Same bare-`cached` import also appears in `utils/chat.py`, `utils/middleware.py`, and `utils/models.py`; audit those too."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 428,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#architecture"
          ],
          "text": "**Redis/valkey + multi-worker \u2014 the actual concurrency lever** (scoped during the caching pass, needs its own plan): `start.sh:233` runs `--workers ${UVICORN_WORKERS:-1}`, so this is a SINGLE-process app today. `REDIS_URL`, `WEBSOCKET_MANAGER`, `WEBSOCKET_REDIS_URL` and a redis-backed `PersistentConfig` all already exist in the codebase, but nothing in the Dockerfile, compose, or CapRover config sets them. Until they are wired, raising `UVICORN_WORKERS` breaks websocket fan-out (each worker holds its own socket state) and duplicates every in-process cache per worker. This \u2014 not HTML caching \u2014 is the memcached-shaped hole in the Django comparison. Needs: a plan, a valkey sidecar decision (in-image vs CapRover one-click vs Sprig\u2122), and a multi-worker test that proves socket events cross workers."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 429,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: put HTTP/2 in front (the other half of the old waterfall item \u2014 parallel boot fetches pay off more once they stop queueing behind HTTP/1.1 connection limits)"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 430,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: `/api/models` alone is ~770\u2013995 ms of a ~820\u20131100 ms boot. INVESTIGATED 2026-07-26, and it is **latency, not payload** \u2014 that measurement was taken with an EMPTY model list, so there is nothing to paginate. The time is spent in `get_all_base_models` (`utils/models.py:60-76`) probing the OpenAI and Ollama providers; it already runs them under one `asyncio.gather`, so the cost is the slowest single probe (a connect timeout in the zero-egress e2e container). Worse, **`ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaults to `False`** (`config.py:994-997`), so every call re-probes every provider \u2014 on a real deployment that is an upstream round-trip per boot, per refresh, per user. Two separate levers, each wanting its own guard-rail and before/after: (a) **backend** \u2014 default the base-models cache on and/or warm it at startup, and bound the per-provider probe timeout; (b) **frontend/islands** \u2014 stop gating `loaded` on it: render the shell, fill the selector when it lands, and make the admin setup-wizard check (which reads `$models.length === 0`) await the promise instead of the boot chain. Pagination is a THIRD, separate lever that only pays off once a payload is measured large on a loaded instance \u2014 same open question for the chat list, tools, prompts, and knowledge, which grow with tenant size."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 431,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e"
          ],
          "text": "**Poka-Yoke pass on the e2e suite \u2014 `make e2e` is GREEN, 15/15 specs, 30/30 tests** (2026-07-27). Three devices, each aimed at the CLASS rather than the instance. **(1) Shared-container config leaks.** The harness boots ONE container per run, so anything a spec writes to server config silently changes the world for every spec after it. It bit twice \u2014 `stt-misconfig` left `STT_ENGINE=openai` behind (so `stt-not-configured` saw 502 where it expects 501; it passed in isolation, which was the tell), and a banner seeded by `boot-waterfall` sat on top of later specs' pages \u2014 and a defect that happens twice is the trigger to change the system rather than be more careful. `support/e2e.ts` now snapshots the admin-mutable config surface (audio, banners, ollama, openai, user-permissions) before each spec and restores ONLY what changed afterwards: specs that touch nothing issue zero writes, so the guard cannot itself become a drift source, and a leak is logged BY NAME so the culprit is visible rather than whichever spec fails next. No future spec author has to remember. **(2) Implicit precondition.** `stt-not-configured` asserted behaviour for \"no STT engine configured\" while merely INHERITING whatever the container happened to hold; it now sets `stt.ENGINE=''` itself, making it true on its own terms and in any order. **(3) The sprigs-panel failure was NOT a leak \u2014 the original diagnosis on this line was wrong.** Corrected by reading the failure screenshot instead of the terminal: the graft SUCCEEDED, `data-state=delivered` passed, and the real failure was `cy.contains('Restart the Rootstock')` matching nothing \u2014 because that phrase no longer exists anywhere in the product. Grafting became restart-free (the `importlib.invalidate_caches()` work) and the note was reworded to \"Vector DB delivered. Document search is active now.\", so a genuine UX improvement surfaced as a failing graft test. Compounding it, Cypress RETRIED a state-mutating test: with vector-chroma already delivered the Graft button is a Prune button, so the retry died with \"sprig-graft never found\" \u2014 and that misleading second error masked the first, sending the diagnosis after an ordering bug that never existed. Both fixed: the assertion now reads `post_graft_note` from `/catalog` and asserts the UI shows THAT (rewording can never break it again, while the contract it guards \u2014 the operator is TOLD what happens next \u2014 is still enforced), and the test is pinned `retries: 0`, because a retry of a mutating test is not a retry, it is a different test run against different state."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 432,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**The e2e harness could silently run on the PREVIOUS run's data** (found 2026-07-29, the more valuable find of the day). `run-cypress.sh` reset itself with `docker rm -f \"$ROOT\"` followed by `docker volume rm \"$VOL\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true`, and that `|| true` was the whole bug: a container that has not finished going away still holds its volume, so the removal fails, and the run then boots on the last run's data **with nothing printed and no non-zero status anywhere**. Same shape as the two gate-quality bugs already on this board \u2014 `docker run` without `-i` checking nothing and exiting 0, and `| grep -q` under pipefail reading a match as a failure \u2014 which is now three instances of one class: _a gate whose failure is indistinguishable from success_. **How it surfaced, and why the symptom is the dangerous part**: two `e2e_both` runs were killed mid-flight (SIGKILL skips the cleanup trap), leaving `sage-e2e` alive; the next run inherited a volume with `vector-chroma` already delivered, and `sprigs-panel` failed hunting a Graft button that had become a Prune button. So the harness reported a plausible-looking failure **on a surface nobody had touched**, which sends the diagnosis to the wrong place \u2014 the spec passes in isolation, so it reads as a flake, and the honest-looking conclusion \"known flaky test\" would have been wrong twice over. Fix: wait for the container to actually be gone, retry the volume removal, and **hard-exit naming the container still holding it** if it survives \u2014 a gate that cannot guarantee its own starting state is not measuring what it claims to and must stop rather than carry on quietly. Proved able to fire, not assumed: pinned the volume with an unrelated container and confirmed the run refuses with `FATAL: volume 'sage-e2e-data' survived removal`, naming `volume-holder` as the culprit. Process note for whoever reads the logs from that day: the first re-run afterwards failed with `expected 502 to be one of [200, 400, 403]`, which was NOT the product \u2014 it was me running the guard's own fixture (`docker rm -f sage-e2e`) concurrently with a live gate and destroying its container mid-run. Do not run docker experiments beside a running gate."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 433,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**\"Would Selenium test the colour picker?\" \u2014 answered, and the answer corrected a claim I had made** (Alexander, 2026-07-29, after a manual regression pass). Short answer: **Selenium buys nothing Cypress does not already have here, and the direction I called human-only was never human-only.** No WebDriver-family tool \u2014 Selenium, Playwright, Cypress \u2014 can open or drive the OS colour dialog, because that dialog is rendered by the browser/OS outside the page. But the dialog is not our code. Our contract is \"when the picker's value changes, the hex field follows\", and a synthetic `input` event exercises exactly that: `color-pair.js` is delegated from the document and listens for `input`, and the Svelte page's `bind:value` reacts to the same event. So `cy.get(picker).invoke('val', '#22aa55').trigger('input')` tests it, and now does \u2014 **green against BOTH implementations, and proved able to fail** by breaking only the picker\u2192hex branch and confirming that one test went red while hex\u2192picker stayed green. I had told Alexander this needed a human; that was overstated and is now fixed rather than left as a standing manual step. What genuinely stays out of reach for every driver is proving the swatch OPENS a dialog at all \u2014 browser behaviour, not ours, and not what breaks. **Where image-based automation (SikuliX) actually earns its place** is a different class entirely, and this project already has the scar: Phase S shipped an autoscroll that went **13/13 green under a real browser driver and was broken on a real trackpad**, because synthetic input models neither trackpad jitter nor touch momentum. That is the gap \u2014 real input physics, OS-level gestures, native dialogs \u2014 not DOM state. So SikuliX is worth keeping on the roadmap for the STREAMING and scroll surfaces (Phases 3\u20134), where the failure mode is physical, and is close to worthless for form surfaces like this one, where a synthetic event reaches the same code the human does. Filed against the existing staging/regression item rather than as a new stack."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 434,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Guard-rail Cypress spec for every area before it's touched"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 435,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "Modernize (or prune) the inherited upstream e2e specs \u2014 `registration/settings/chat/documents.cy.ts` now COMPILE (import depth `../support`\u2192`../../support` fixed) but FAIL on stale open-webui UI: they assume OPEN signup (this fork hard-closes it after the first admin, so the \"Sign up\" toggle is hidden) and the old changelog dismiss `Okay, Let's Go!` (now the setup wizard's \"Get Started\"/Close, handled via API). Rewrite to the fork's actual flows or drop tests of removed behavior. Note: they're in a subdir so NOT in the default `make e2e` gate."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0da79273cadfedc9",
      "line_num": 412,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phase Q \u2014 clear the decks: current-SPA drift fixes, cruft, and perf quick-wins (no migration needed).**\n- [x] Enforce the 8-char password server-side (`auths.py`) \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (200\u2192bug, 400\u2192fixed); committed\n- [x] Fix `UserUpdateForm` / `SpaceForm` TS\u2194Pydantic drift \u2014 committed\n- [x] Delete the dead `SUPPORTED_FILE_EXTENSIONS` copy in `constants.ts` (backend 50-entry list is live) \u2014 committed\n- [x] **Reconcile the drifted permission-tree defaults** (2026-07-27) \u2014 the backend described user permissions in TWO independent places and nothing kept them in agreement: the env-derived `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS` table (`config.py`, what `has_permission()` ENFORCES) and hardcoded Pydantic model defaults (`routers/users.py`, what the admin panel ADVERTISES). Diffed them exactly by importing both inside the image: **4 value disagreements** (`sharing.public_models/public_knowledge/public_prompts/public_tools` \u2014 env `False`, Pydantic `True`) and **1 key missing entirely** (`features.web_search` existed only in Pydantic). The 4 sharing ones are LATENT \u2014 masked because the persisted `USER_PERMISSIONS` carries those keys, so stored values override the Pydantic defaults \u2014 but they are a loaded gun for any future key. `features.web_search` was live and user-visible: the panel advertised it on, enforcement read the absent key as denied, and `MessageInput.svelte:469-470` gates the web-search button on `$user.permissions.features.web_search`, so **no non-admin ever got the button while the panel said they had it**. Fixes: (a) added `USER_PERMISSIONS_FEATURES_WEB_SEARCH` (default `True`, matching what the panel always advertised and the sibling features; exposure stays gated by the global `enable_web_search` flag, so this switches nothing on for deployments that have not enabled web search) and wired it into the table; (b) the Pydantic model defaults now DERIVE from `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS` instead of restating it, so the panel cannot advertise a policy the backend does not apply \u2014 and a Pydantic field with no table entry now raises `KeyError` at import, i.e. the container fails loudly at boot instead of shipping a silent policy lie; (c) `get_permissions()` (`utils/access_control.py`) now backfills from `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS` as well as from the saved config \u2014 without this the fix would never reach an UPGRADED deployment, whose stored dict predates the key, leaving `has_permission()` allowing it while the payload the frontend renders omitted it. Guard-rail `permissions-drift.cy.ts` asserts the observable contract rather than the internal tables: every advertised key must be enforced, values must match, and `features.web_search` is pinned by name. Measure-twice: RED pre-change (2 of 3 failing, `features.web_search advertised but not enforced`), GREEN after. Deliberately ONE-directional \u2014 a v1.1.1 database still carries a retired `workspace` group from before this fork renamed it `workshop`, so asserting equality would fail on every upgraded deployment for no reason. **Verified on real legacy data**: booted this image on a copy of the v1.1.1 prod snapshot via `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` (20/21, see below) and compared both endpoints directly \u2014 `features` identical on both sides including `web_search: true`. That legacy check was done by direct API comparison, not through the Cypress spec, because the Cypress container and the gate's container are not on a shared docker network. Frontend note: all 13 `?? true`/`?? false` permission defaults in `src/` already MATCH the backend (checked mechanically, 86 permission reads total) \u2014 no frontend change needed, though those fallbacks are now dead weight that could mask future drift, filed below. Gates: `make e2e` 13/15, only the two known pre-existing leaks failing. Committed. #security #critical\n- [ ] **Drop the frontend's permission fallbacks** (follow-up to the reconcile above): the 13 `$user?.permissions?.x?.y ?? true` sites in `src/` all match the backend today, and now that every enforced key is guaranteed present in the payload they are unreachable defaults. Worse, they can only ever mask future drift \u2014 a `?? true` renders a control the API will refuse. Delete them so the frontend reads what the backend sent, with a guard-rail that a denied permission hides its control. #frontend #tech-debt\n- [x] **RESOLVED \u2014 it was never a product bug: `| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`** (2026-07-28). The check greps the target-arch container's logs for `Sprig\u2122 host architecture: amd64`, and it was recorded as either a normalization bug or an emulation quirk. It was neither. **`writer | grep -q PATTERN` under pipefail reports a MATCH as a FAILURE**: grep exits on the first hit, the writer still has output queued, takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, and pipefail surfaces 141 as the pipeline's status. It only fires when the writer had more to say, which is why it looked like flakiness \u2014 the same assertion passes on a quiet container and fails on a busy one. Reproduced deterministically (200k-line writer \u2192 status 141 on a match, five runs out of five). **The same bug cost a second gate**: `sprig_signing`'s \"minisign OK\" check failed inside `gauntlet_full` and passed in isolation, and I nearly filed it as a flake. **And it had a worse third form nobody had noticed**: `upgrade-gate.sh:55` asserts the ABSENCE of a traceback in the migration logs \u2014 there, 141 reads as \"no match\", so the gate reported clean on exactly the failure it exists to catch. That check could not have caught a migration traceback on production data. Devices, in preference order: (1) pipe-free primitives in `scripts/lib/gate.sh` \u2014 `text_has`, `log_has`, `log_has_i`, `wait_for_log`, `fetch_has` \u2014 which capture the text and match with bash's own operators, so there is no writer to kill and no status to misread; (2) every unbounded-writer site converted (2 `docker logs` in the gates, 1 in `wizard-smoke.sh`, 8 `curl` in the two smoke gates, 1 in `build-sprig-tika.sh`); (3) `make pipefail_lint` refuses the shape returning, verified to FAIL on a reintroduced line and pass once removed; (4) `make pipefail_fixture` proves BOTH halves \u2014 that the trap is real AND that the helpers fix it \u2014 because a device that fixes nothing looks identical to one that works unless the broken shape is asserted too. Both wired into `gauntlet_full`; the lint also gates `make lint`. Scope is deliberate and written into the lint: only `docker logs` and `curl` writers are banned, because `printf`/`echo` are builtins on in-memory strings that finish before grep can exit \u2014 widen it if a `printf` haystack ever passes 64 KB. Verified after: `gauntlet_full` exit 0 (fixture 7/7, smoke 66/66, durability 12/12, signing 10/10, ui-Sprig 22/22, parity green, e2e 46 passing), `upgrade_gate` **21/21**. #critical\n- [x] Cruft sweep (deps): removed unused `chart.js` + `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` from package.json (zero code refs \u2014 grep + a clean vite build both confirm) and the dead `DOMPurify` import in `MarkdownInlineTokens.svelte`. bun.lock reconciled (`Removed: 2`); `it_build` frozen-install + build green; markdown/sanitizer guard-rails green. (A fuller depcheck of the other 100+ deps is a separate pass.) Committed.\n- [x] Strip hyperscript attribute forms (`_`, `script`, `data-script`) in the user-content sanitizer \u2014 one app-wide DOMPurify `uponSanitizeAttribute` hook (`utils/sanitize.ts`, installed in root `+layout.svelte`) covers all 14 `@html` sites; `data-script` survived DOMPurify's default `ALLOW_DATA_ATTR`, the other two were already dropped. Defense-in-depth (hyperscript not yet loaded). Guard-rail `content-security-sanitizer.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-fix (data-script leaked via a rendered banner), GREEN post. Committed.\n- [x] Perf: `Cache-Control: immutable` on `_app/immutable/*` \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (empty header\u2192immutable); committed\n- [x] Perf: eager ML libs off the conversation chunk (nodes/2) \u2014 MEASURED: the 22 MB WASM was already lazy (feature-gated, NOT on the load path; the audit inferred wrong). Real win: deleted dead `@huggingface/transformers` import + made kokoro-js dynamic in `chat/Settings/Audio.svelte`. Fresh-build verified: nodes/2 2.70\u21920.65 MB, app load 5.39\u21924.12 MB transfer (\u221224%), 13.4\u219210.6 MB decoded; auth+cache guard-rails green. Committed.\n- [x] Perf: `/icons/` waste \u2014 RESOLVED as already-gated. Image ships 0 `.xcf` (`.dockerignore **/*.xcf`) and 1.3 MB icons total; the audit read the stale host build. The 6 identical 170 KB PNGs are placeholder art meant to diverge (favicon/logo/splash + dark variants) \u2192 branding-art task, not perf.\n- [x] Perf/arch: lazy-load markdown-render libs (katex/mermaid/highlight/codemirror) \u2014 SHIPPED as architecture hardening, MEASURED as perf-neutral. Four changes landed: (A) deleted the DEAD `import katex` in `utils/marked/katex-extension.ts` (tokenizer never used it; real render is `KatexRenderer.svelte`, untouched \u2192 tex still renders); (B) `hljs` now dynamic-imported inside `copyToClipboard` (copy-only, off every page's utils chunk); (C+D) `CodeBlock` + `KatexRenderer` load via memoized `{#await import()}` loaders (`Markdown/lazy.ts`) only when a code/math token renders \u2014 streaming-safe (one promise identity, no re-pending flash). MEASURE-TWICE FINDING: the hoped-for \"plain convo sheds ~10 MB\" is NOT real \u2014 mermaid/codemirror/katex were ALREADY lazy (Vite component chunking); a plain convo never pulled them, before or after (measured: release-3.0.0 vs develop, identical 13.41\u219210.27 delta = the nodes/2 win alone; no separate mermaid/codemirror chunk fetched in EITHER build). So this change's value is real but not bytes: dead-code removal, hljs deferral, and EXPLICIT/structural laziness aligned with the plan's islands principle (robust vs a future refactor making them eager). Guard-rail `markdown-render-lazy.cy.ts` GREEN before AND after (seeds a chat, asserts `.katex` + `.copy-code-button` + `pre.mermaid` render; plain-text control asserts none) \u2014 proves the refactor broke no rendering. #perf #frontend\n- [x] Perf: batch the serial boot waterfall into `Promise.all` \u2014 the `(app)/+layout.svelte` boot ladder awaited `getModels` \u2192 `getBanners` \u2192 `getTools` \u2192 `getToolServersData` one at a time, though only the first depends on anything (user settings, for `directConnections`). Now one wave after settings. MEASURED (fresh container, `boot-waterfall-timing.json`): BEFORE the three were strictly disjoint \u2014 models `272.7\u21921041.3`, banners `1043.9\u21921050.2`, tools `1052.8\u21921060.3`; AFTER all three start within 1 ms of each other and banners/tools finish well inside the models window. Local saving is small in absolute terms (~19 ms, since banners/tools are ~7 ms each on loopback) but it is three round-trips removed, so the win scales with RTT \u2014 on a 60 ms link that is ~180 ms off first paint. Guard-rail `boot-waterfall.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-change (last start 1153.1 ms vs first end 1139.7 ms \u2014 no overlap), GREEN after. The spec also pins the dependency that must SURVIVE: settings still resolves before `/api/models` is issued. Committed. #perf #frontend\n- [x] **Perf: caching pass** (2026-07-26) \u2014 surveyed every layer that could take a cache, then fixed the two that paid. Framing first, because it decides everything else: the \"$5 VPS serves 100k concurrent\" pattern comes from caching *anonymous identical HTML*, and this app has almost none \u2014 nearly every response is per-user authenticated, plus streaming chat over websockets. **(1) Base-models cache, the big one.** `/api/models` probes every configured provider on every call. MEASURED live against the shipped image: uncached **~740 ms**, cached **~4.5 ms** (~160\u00d7), and `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaulted to `False`. Flipped the default to `True` (`config.py`), which also engages the existing startup warm (`main.py:732`) so even the first request is cheap. The reason it had been off is that the cache has NO TTL and nothing cleared it \u2014 so added `invalidate_base_models_cache()` (`utils/models.py`) and called it from both provider-config update endpoints (`routers/openai.py`, `routers/ollama.py`; imported at call site because `utils.models` imports both routers and a top-level import closes the cycle). Custom models need no invalidation \u2014 they merge fresh on top of the cached base list every call. Guard-rail `models-cache.cy.ts` asserts BOTH halves, because a fast cache with broken invalidation is worse than the slow path: median `/api/models` under 250 ms, AND a provider-config write forces a re-probe, AND the cache refills after. Measure-twice: RED pre-change (median **749 ms**), GREEN after. **(2) `/static` and `/assets` shipped no `Cache-Control`** \u2014 verified live with curl: ETag only, so every repeat visit spends a revalidation round-trip per asset. Added `CachedStaticFiles` (`main.py`) with a BOUNDED `public, max-age=604800`, deliberately not `immutable`: these filenames are reused across deploys (STATIC_DIR re-syncs from the build at startup), so a week is the worst-case staleness. Theme CSS is untouched \u2014 it has its own route with an explicit `no-cache`. `static-caching.cy.ts` grew two tests (bounded-but-not-immutable on `/static`, and the SPA shell must NOT carry a long max-age); RED pre-change on the new one. **COMBINED RESULT, same spec, same harness: boot data-fetch span 821.5 ms \u2192 102.5 ms (8\u00d7), the fetch wave itself 787.6 ms \u2192 21.6 ms, `/api/models` 768 ms \u2192 17 ms.** Gates: `make e2e` 12/14, only the two known pre-existing leaks failing. Caveat for upgrades: `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` is a `PersistentConfig`, so a deployment where an admin explicitly saved this setting keeps their saved choice; everyone else picks up the new default. Committed. #perf\n- [ ] **`@cached(ttl=MODELS_CACHE_TTL)` never hits \u2014 the decorator is inert** (found during the caching pass, 2026-07-26): `routers/openai.py:550` and `routers/ollama.py:378` use bare `aiocache.cached` with no `key_builder`. aiocache's default key is built from `str(args)`, and the first arg is a Starlette `Request` \u2014 a new object with a new address on every call \u2014 so the key never repeats. PROVEN: with `MODELS_CACHE_TTL=60`, five back-to-back `/api/models` calls took 0.74 / 0.73 / 0.78 / 1.70 / 0.74 s. Zero hits. Two consequences: `MODELS_CACHE_TTL` is a knob that does nothing, and every request stores an entry held for the TTL, so memory grows with request rate. Fix is a `key_builder` keyed on stable values (user id + provider-config revision) \u2014 or delete the decorators, since removing something that never hits is behaviour-preserving. Same bare-`cached` import also appears in `utils/chat.py`, `utils/middleware.py`, and `utils/models.py`; audit those too. #perf #tech-debt\n- [ ] **Redis/valkey + multi-worker \u2014 the actual concurrency lever** (scoped during the caching pass, needs its own plan): `start.sh:233` runs `--workers ${UVICORN_WORKERS:-1}`, so this is a SINGLE-process app today. `REDIS_URL`, `WEBSOCKET_MANAGER`, `WEBSOCKET_REDIS_URL` and a redis-backed `PersistentConfig` all already exist in the codebase, but nothing in the Dockerfile, compose, or CapRover config sets them. Until they are wired, raising `UVICORN_WORKERS` breaks websocket fan-out (each worker holds its own socket state) and duplicates every in-process cache per worker. This \u2014 not HTML caching \u2014 is the memcached-shaped hole in the Django comparison. Needs: a plan, a valkey sidecar decision (in-image vs CapRover one-click vs Sprig\u2122), and a multi-worker test that proves socket events cross workers. #perf #architecture\n- [ ] Perf: put HTTP/2 in front (the other half of the old waterfall item \u2014 parallel boot fetches pay off more once they stop queueing behind HTTP/1.1 connection limits) #perf\n- [ ] Perf: `/api/models` alone is ~770\u2013995 ms of a ~820\u20131100 ms boot. INVESTIGATED 2026-07-26, and it is **latency, not payload** \u2014 that measurement was taken with an EMPTY model list, so there is nothing to paginate. The time is spent in `get_all_base_models` (`utils/models.py:60-76`) probing the OpenAI and Ollama providers; it already runs them under one `asyncio.gather`, so the cost is the slowest single probe (a connect timeout in the zero-egress e2e container). Worse, **`ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaults to `False`** (`config.py:994-997`), so every call re-probes every provider \u2014 on a real deployment that is an upstream round-trip per boot, per refresh, per user. Two separate levers, each wanting its own guard-rail and before/after: (a) **backend** \u2014 default the base-models cache on and/or warm it at startup, and bound the per-provider probe timeout; (b) **frontend/islands** \u2014 stop gating `loaded` on it: render the shell, fill the selector when it lands, and make the admin setup-wizard check (which reads `$models.length === 0`) await the promise instead of the boot chain. Pagination is a THIRD, separate lever that only pays off once a payload is measured large on a loaded instance \u2014 same open question for the chat list, tools, prompts, and knowledge, which grow with tenant size. #perf\n- [x] **Poka-Yoke pass on the e2e suite \u2014 `make e2e` is GREEN, 15/15 specs, 30/30 tests** (2026-07-27). Three devices, each aimed at the CLASS rather than the instance. **(1) Shared-container config leaks.** The harness boots ONE container per run, so anything a spec writes to server config silently changes the world for every spec after it. It bit twice \u2014 `stt-misconfig` left `STT_ENGINE=openai` behind (so `stt-not-configured` saw 502 where it expects 501; it passed in isolation, which was the tell), and a banner seeded by `boot-waterfall` sat on top of later specs' pages \u2014 and a defect that happens twice is the trigger to change the system rather than be more careful. `support/e2e.ts` now snapshots the admin-mutable config surface (audio, banners, ollama, openai, user-permissions) before each spec and restores ONLY what changed afterwards: specs that touch nothing issue zero writes, so the guard cannot itself become a drift source, and a leak is logged BY NAME so the culprit is visible rather than whichever spec fails next. No future spec author has to remember. **(2) Implicit precondition.** `stt-not-configured` asserted behaviour for \"no STT engine configured\" while merely INHERITING whatever the container happened to hold; it now sets `stt.ENGINE=''` itself, making it true on its own terms and in any order. **(3) The sprigs-panel failure was NOT a leak \u2014 the original diagnosis on this line was wrong.** Corrected by reading the failure screenshot instead of the terminal: the graft SUCCEEDED, `data-state=delivered` passed, and the real failure was `cy.contains('Restart the Rootstock')` matching nothing \u2014 because that phrase no longer exists anywhere in the product. Grafting became restart-free (the `importlib.invalidate_caches()` work) and the note was reworded to \"Vector DB delivered. Document search is active now.\", so a genuine UX improvement surfaced as a failing graft test. Compounding it, Cypress RETRIED a state-mutating test: with vector-chroma already delivered the Graft button is a Prune button, so the retry died with \"sprig-graft never found\" \u2014 and that misleading second error masked the first, sending the diagnosis after an ordering bug that never existed. Both fixed: the assertion now reads `post_graft_note` from `/catalog` and asserts the UI shows THAT (rewording can never break it again, while the contract it guards \u2014 the operator is TOLD what happens next \u2014 is still enforced), and the test is pinned `retries: 0`, because a retry of a mutating test is not a retry, it is a different test run against different state. #e2e\n- [x] **The e2e harness could silently run on the PREVIOUS run's data** (found 2026-07-29, the more valuable find of the day). `run-cypress.sh` reset itself with `docker rm -f \"$ROOT\"` followed by `docker volume rm \"$VOL\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true`, and that `|| true` was the whole bug: a container that has not finished going away still holds its volume, so the removal fails, and the run then boots on the last run's data **with nothing printed and no non-zero status anywhere**. Same shape as the two gate-quality bugs already on this board \u2014 `docker run` without `-i` checking nothing and exiting 0, and `| grep -q` under pipefail reading a match as a failure \u2014 which is now three instances of one class: _a gate whose failure is indistinguishable from success_. **How it surfaced, and why the symptom is the dangerous part**: two `e2e_both` runs were killed mid-flight (SIGKILL skips the cleanup trap), leaving `sage-e2e` alive; the next run inherited a volume with `vector-chroma` already delivered, and `sprigs-panel` failed hunting a Graft button that had become a Prune button. So the harness reported a plausible-looking failure **on a surface nobody had touched**, which sends the diagnosis to the wrong place \u2014 the spec passes in isolation, so it reads as a flake, and the honest-looking conclusion \"known flaky test\" would have been wrong twice over. Fix: wait for the container to actually be gone, retry the volume removal, and **hard-exit naming the container still holding it** if it survives \u2014 a gate that cannot guarantee its own starting state is not measuring what it claims to and must stop rather than carry on quietly. Proved able to fire, not assumed: pinned the volume with an unrelated container and confirmed the run refuses with `FATAL: volume 'sage-e2e-data' survived removal`, naming `volume-holder` as the culprit. Process note for whoever reads the logs from that day: the first re-run afterwards failed with `expected 502 to be one of [200, 400, 403]`, which was NOT the product \u2014 it was me running the guard's own fixture (`docker rm -f sage-e2e`) concurrently with a live gate and destroying its container mid-run. Do not run docker experiments beside a running gate. #e2e #critical\n- [x] **\"Would Selenium test the colour picker?\" \u2014 answered, and the answer corrected a claim I had made** (Alexander, 2026-07-29, after a manual regression pass). Short answer: **Selenium buys nothing Cypress does not already have here, and the direction I called human-only was never human-only.** No WebDriver-family tool \u2014 Selenium, Playwright, Cypress \u2014 can open or drive the OS colour dialog, because that dialog is rendered by the browser/OS outside the page. But the dialog is not our code. Our contract is \"when the picker's value changes, the hex field follows\", and a synthetic `input` event exercises exactly that: `color-pair.js` is delegated from the document and listens for `input`, and the Svelte page's `bind:value` reacts to the same event. So `cy.get(picker).invoke('val', '#22aa55').trigger('input')` tests it, and now does \u2014 **green against BOTH implementations, and proved able to fail** by breaking only the picker\u2192hex branch and confirming that one test went red while hex\u2192picker stayed green. I had told Alexander this needed a human; that was overstated and is now fixed rather than left as a standing manual step. What genuinely stays out of reach for every driver is proving the swatch OPENS a dialog at all \u2014 browser behaviour, not ours, and not what breaks. **Where image-based automation (SikuliX) actually earns its place** is a different class entirely, and this project already has the scar: Phase S shipped an autoscroll that went **13/13 green under a real browser driver and was broken on a real trackpad**, because synthetic input models neither trackpad jitter nor touch momentum. That is the gap \u2014 real input physics, OS-level gestures, native dialogs \u2014 not DOM state. So SikuliX is worth keeping on the roadmap for the STREAMING and scroll surfaces (Phases 3\u20134), where the failure mode is physical, and is close to worthless for form surfaces like this one, where a synthetic event reaches the same code the human does. Filed against the existing staging/regression item rather than as a new stack. #e2e #frontend\n- [ ] Guard-rail Cypress spec for every area before it's touched\n- [ ] Modernize (or prune) the inherited upstream e2e specs \u2014 `registration/settings/chat/documents.cy.ts` now COMPILE (import depth `../support`\u2192`../../support` fixed) but FAIL on stale open-webui UI: they assume OPEN signup (this fork hard-closes it after the first admin, so the \"Sign up\" toggle is hidden) and the old changelog dismiss `Okay, Let's Go!` (now the setup wizard's \"Get Started\"/Close, handled via API). Rewrite to the fork's actual flows or drop tests of removed behavior. Note: they're in a subdir so NOT in the default `make e2e` gate. #e2e #tech-debt\n\n`TODO.md:L412`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5550
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "15d79bc5f8d9acde",
      "line_num": 457,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#ux"
      ],
      "text": "**Human UX review of the no-build surfaces before any of them takes over a route (Alexander, 2026-07-28: \"it's close but the UX feels a little off\"). Two surfaces exist in both forms right now \u2014 `/admin/sprigs` vs `/pages/admin/sprigs`, `/admin/diagnostics` vs `/pages/admin/diagnostics` \u2014 which makes this the cheapest it will ever be to judge: same data, same deployment, flip between them. `scripts/manual-check.sh` boots a seeded instance with both live (`--graft-ui` for the marketplace slot). This is not a nice-to-have: Phase S established that a green suite is the WEAKEST evidence on an interactive surface (an autoscroll went 13/13 green and was broken on a real trackpad), and this phase has already produced a second instance of the same thing \u2014 the \"Show me how to fix this\" button rendered and did nothing, every automated assertion passed, and a human click found it in seconds. Known feel-differences to judge rather than assume: messages are CSS toasts fixed to the top instead of a toast library; the fix remedy is a `<details>` disclosure instead of a modal; the diagnostics timestamp is absolute instead of a live relative label; the Sprigs panel swaps whole rather than patching; the branding preview reflects SAVED values rather than updating as you type. Its colour pair is now tested in BOTH directions (see below), so what a human adds there is narrower than first filed: whether the swatch opens the OS dialog at all, and whether picking a colour in it feels immediate. Scope it as a real review with someone who has not been building it.**\n`#frontend` `#ux`\n`TODO.md:L457`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5640
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 515,
          "tags": [
            "#biomes",
            "#ratchet"
          ],
          "text": "Count and pin the core biomes as a downward-only ratchet \u2014 chat core, rich-text editor, code editor, flow canvas, channels; the count only decreases"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 516,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Mid-term: rebuild the chat core as an htmx flow, not a ported island** (Alexander, 2026-07-27). The plan currently treats the chat core as the worst-value surface \u2014 17,591 lines for a 15\u201330% cut \u2014 on the assumption it gets ported to islands, where app-shaped code changes dialect rather than disappearing. An htmx-first rebuild is a different bet: the server owns the conversation and sends fragments, and only the token stream stays vanilla (Phase S already proved that half in ~260 lines, abort semantics included). Two Phase 0 findings point the same way \u2014 the island path did NOT shrink a form surface while the fragment path is the one the estimates assumed, and the runtime probe showed a biome is a real boundary to work across, not a free wrapper. Prerequisites before this is more than a direction: the Phase 1 cookie bridge (fragments need the token off localStorage), the non-proxying browser driver Phase S made a condition, and a fragment surface actually measured. Do NOT re-plan the phase order on this yet \u2014 it is a hypothesis with two supporting datapoints, and the thing that would settle it is one migrated fragment surface."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 517,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Reimplement the flow canvas (`@xyflow/svelte`) framework-free"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 518,
          "tags": [
            "#brand"
          ],
          "text": "Slogan precision: \"org-owned\" not \"user-owned\"; keep yjs local-first for the editor"
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "43129a062069b985",
      "line_num": 514,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phases 3\u20134 \u2014 medium surfaces, shell flip, then biomes: users, evals, settings tabs, chat list, workshop \u2192 prune biomes (Functions, then chat core last, gated on the spike memo).**\n- [ ] Count and pin the core biomes as a downward-only ratchet \u2014 chat core, rich-text editor, code editor, flow canvas, channels; the count only decreases #biomes #ratchet\n- [ ] **Mid-term: rebuild the chat core as an htmx flow, not a ported island** (Alexander, 2026-07-27). The plan currently treats the chat core as the worst-value surface \u2014 17,591 lines for a 15\u201330% cut \u2014 on the assumption it gets ported to islands, where app-shaped code changes dialect rather than disappearing. An htmx-first rebuild is a different bet: the server owns the conversation and sends fragments, and only the token stream stays vanilla (Phase S already proved that half in ~260 lines, abort semantics included). Two Phase 0 findings point the same way \u2014 the island path did NOT shrink a form surface while the fragment path is the one the estimates assumed, and the runtime probe showed a biome is a real boundary to work across, not a free wrapper. Prerequisites before this is more than a direction: the Phase 1 cookie bridge (fragments need the token off localStorage), the non-proxying browser driver Phase S made a condition, and a fragment surface actually measured. Do NOT re-plan the phase order on this yet \u2014 it is a hypothesis with two supporting datapoints, and the thing that would settle it is one migrated fragment surface. #frontend\n- [ ] Reimplement the flow canvas (`@xyflow/svelte`) framework-free\n- [ ] Slogan precision: \"org-owned\" not \"user-owned\"; keep yjs local-first for the editor #brand\n\n`TODO.md:L514`",
