      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f8e1d8af4557bca5",
      "line_num": 711,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#critical",
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**Chat Microphone Recording Does Not Populate Message Input: Recording from the microphone icon in chat does not process speech into the text field used to send messages**\n- [ ] Reproduce the issue in the chat interface and confirm whether capture, transcription, or input binding is failing\n- [ ] Trace the microphone/transcription flow from recorder output into the chat composer state\n- [ ] Fix the handoff so completed recordings populate the message input field\n- [ ] Add regression coverage for microphone-to-input behavior in chat\n\n`#critical` `#bug`\n`TODO.md:L711`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 150
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "1f34dffa2547da5c",
      "line_num": 459,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug",
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**Inline code in the release notes stopped rendering as code \u2014 fixed at the parser (2026-08-01; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 166 tests/131 passing/35 pending, nobuild 166/166 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Alexander, from the live wizard: a note reading ``Each recipe (`scripts/build-sprig-*.sh`) runs its sanity gate`` arrived as `Each recipe ( scripts/build-sprig-*.sh ) runs its sanity gate` \u2014 no monospace, and stray spaces inside the brackets. Not caused by the Jinja conversion: `env.py` renders CHANGELOG.md to HTML and then flattens each entry by walking its children, calling `get_text(strip=True)` on each, and joining with a space \u2014 so every inline element was replaced by its bare text with padding either side. It had been that way for as long as the parser has existed, and it also affected `/api/changelog`. Fixed at the source rather than per-consumer: the prose branch now clones the paragraph, drops the `<strong>` title, and takes the rest as ONE string. Measured against the shipped CHANGELOG.md before changing it \u2014 40 of the 71 entries carrying inline code lose padding, none gains a double space, and none runs two words together. `changelog_panel.py` then rebuilds the `<code>` elements from the `raw` HTML the parser already kept, as runs rather than HTML, so the template stays autoescaped and there is no `| safe` anywhere in that path. New gate `changelog-code-spans.cy.ts`, three assertions because any one alone can pass while the bug is present: `<code>` elements exist, no code span carries padding and no bracket is followed by whitespace-then-code, and the entry title is still stripped from its own body. Proved able to fail by restoring the flattening \u2014 two of the three go red. The wizard-changelog guard-rail caught the half-fix, which is the useful part: it asserts the page contains the API's own `content` verbatim, so fixing only the render made the page MORE correct than the API and the spec went red naming exactly that gap. Fixing the parser made both agree.**\n`#bug` `#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L459`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 240
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "3cdbc2c8b705d105",
      "line_num": 461,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**The page reloaded itself every 60 seconds \u2014 the reconnect was a proxy, not the fact (2026-08-01; Alexander: _\"Why does the page refresh every so often now?\"_). Caused by the fix directly above. Bounding the stream to stop it blocking shutdown meant the browser reconnected every minute, and the client reloaded on ANY reconnect \u2014 so it reloaded on a timer. The client's rule was only ever a proxy for \"the server restarted\", and it was already wrong for a dropped connection that comes back to the same process; bounding the stream just made the wrongness visible every minute instead of never. Replaced with the fact: every stream now opens with `event: hello` carrying a token generated at module import, and the client reloads only when that token CHANGES. Same token means the same process whatever happened to the connection. Deliberately not `env.INSTANCE_ID`, which an operator can pin through the environment \u2014 pinning it would silently disable reloading altogether. `reload_gate` grew check 6, both halves, because either alone passes on a constant: two streams from one process must AGREE, and the token must DIFFER after a reload. Proved able to fail by making the token per-connection \u2014 the gate goes red naming the symptom, _\"every reconnect would reload\"_.**\n`#bug`\n`TODO.md:L461`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 330
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f2ad184cb9a539cd",
      "line_num": 463,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug",
        "#critical-gate"
      ],
      "text": "**An open dev-reload stream blocked the reload it exists to announce (2026-08-01, found by live test). Left a tab open on a `/pages/` route, edited a panel, and the log said `WatchFiles detected changes\u2026 Reloading\u2026` and then nothing \u2014 forever, with the original worker still alive twenty minutes later. The SSE endpoint is a long-lived response and uvicorn waits indefinitely for in-flight responses before a reload completes, so the dev-reload connection wedged the dev reload. Two halves to the fix: the stream now ends itself after about a minute (the browser reconnects anyway \u2014 `retry: 1000`), and the reload branch passes `--timeout-graceful-shutdown 3` as the backstop for anything else that streams. `reload_gate` grew check 5 \u2014 open a stream, THEN edit a panel, and require the edit to land \u2014 because the gate ran its stream check and its reload check in an order that never overlapped them, and stayed green through the whole defect. The first version of that check was itself the bug it was written for: it only required the edit to land eventually, and with the defect reinstated it landed in 45.3s and PASSED, because the client's own `--max-time` finally closed the stream. It asserts the elapsed time now \u2014 healthy is ~5s, broken is ~45s \u2014 and goes red naming the cause.**\n`#bug` `#critical-gate`\n`TODO.md:L463`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 420
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "1838d9bfb31980c1",
      "line_num": 677,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**The changelog pager spec passes whether the button moves or not `#critical-gate`: `wizard-changelog.cy.ts` \u2192 \"moves to the other side once the notes run out\" measures `getBoundingClientRect().left` and requires travel greater than half the row. It was green against the transform version, which a human could see was broken, and it is green against the `margin-left` version, which works. A test that reports the same result in both states measured neither. This is the \"failure indistinguishable from success\" shape the repo keeps finding, and it is the fourth instance. The assertion is not obviously wrong \u2014 forcing the shift to 0 did make it RED (`expected 0 to be above 367.5`), so it has teeth against _that_ input. What it did not catch is a transform that applied under the driver and not in the operator's browser, which means the gap is the environment, not the assertion. Decide which: make the check discriminate in a real engine, or drop the claim and say the spec covers paging and the label only. Related: SikuliX is on the roadmap for exactly this class.**\n`#bug`\n`TODO.md:L677`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 510
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "bc6cedad01c3c135",
      "line_num": 679,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**The diagnostics page still renders English for every reader: every other no-build panel now binds `translator(request)` and answers in the reader's language, but `diagnostics_panel.py` calls `t(key, {})` with the default locale at [line 81](app/backend/sage_is_ai/pages/diagnostics_panel.py#L81), [88](app/backend/sage_is_ai/pages/diagnostics_panel.py#L88), [89](app/backend/sage_is_ai/pages/diagnostics_panel.py#L89) and [144](app/backend/sage_is_ai/pages/diagnostics_panel.py#L144). Its keys are the nested `diagnostics.fix.*` ones whose values are real sentences, so this is a genuine loss for a Spanish operator rather than a no-op. Fixing it means threading a locale through `_library_block` \u2192 `_fix_steps` \u2192 `_row` \u2192 `_ghost_block`, four signatures deep, which is why it was not done alongside the wizard panels. The mechanism is already there; only the plumbing is missing.**\n`#bug`\n`TODO.md:L679`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 600
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "df5d7bb3fc51524f",
      "line_num": 681,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug",
        "#a11y"
      ],
      "text": "**`SensitiveInput` hardcodes `id=\"password-input\"`, so a page with two of them mislabels every field but the first: [SensitiveInput.svelte:27](app/src/lib/components/common/SensitiveInput.svelte#L27) sets a literal `id`, and line 23 pairs it with `<label class=\"sr-only\" for=\"password-input\">`. `OAuthSettings.svelte` mounts five of them \u2014 Google secret, GitHub secret, Microsoft secret, magic-link SMTP password, LDAP password \u2014 so one page carries five elements with the same `id`. Duplicate IDs are invalid HTML, and `for` resolves to the first match, so every screen reader announces \"Client Secret\" for all five and clicking any label focuses the Google field. Fix by defaulting `id` to a generated value and letting callers override, the same shape `Switch.svelte` already uses (`export let id = ''`). Found while hooking the panel for the no-build migration, not introduced by it.**\n`#bug` `#a11y`\n`TODO.md:L681`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 690
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "c5f96d90b20a3858",
      "line_num": 683,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**An evicted Sprig\u2122 is reported as \"exited on boot\", which is not what happened: graft two cultivars of the same capability close together and the second one's arrival prunes the first \u2014 `SprigSupervisor.graft` ([supervisor.py:1271-1278](app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/supervisor.py#L1271-L1278)) deliberately keeps one cultivar per capability. The victim is SIGTERMed mid-boot and surfaces as `sprig 'minilm-onnx-inhoused' exited on boot (rc=-15)`, which reads as a crash in the cultivar. `rc=-15` IS SIGTERM, and the supervisor sent it. Hit during the 2026-07-30 manual review by grafting from the wizard while a weight download was still running. Two fixes worth making together: (1) say what happened \u2014 \"pruned while booting: 'multilingual-e5-large' took over the embedding capability\" \u2014 since a message that names everything except the reason is the defect shape this repo keeps finding; (2) child `stderr` is `DEVNULL` ([supervisor.py:1299](app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/supervisor.py#L1299)) with a comment saying logs are not captured yet, so a cultivar that dies for any *other* reason leaves no evidence at all. The wizard panel now refuses to graft a component whose download is in flight, which closes the path that triggered it \u2014 it does not fix the message.**\n`#bug`\n`TODO.md:L683`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 780
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f415b7881106bbb4",
      "line_num": 702,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**AI Engine Wizard Embedding Download Has No Stall Watchdog: When the embedding model fetch from HuggingFace stalls (network drop, HF outage, slow link), the wizard sits in `embedding=downloading` indefinitely. No timeout, no retry, no resumable state surfaced to the admin. `wizard-smoke.sh` catches this externally via `INSTALL_TIMEOUT_SEC`, but a real user has no signal except an idle spinner.**\n`#bug`\n`TODO.md:L702`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 870
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 705,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Surface HF download progress (bytes, last-byte timestamp) to `request.app.state.MODEL_DOWNLOAD_STATUS` so the status endpoint exposes liveness."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 706,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Watchdog in `_download` (`retrieval.py`): if cache size hasn't grown in N minutes (configurable, default 5), mark status=`stalled`, capture the error, allow retry via a re-POST to `/api/v1/retrieval/models/download`."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 707,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Surface stalled state in the wizard UI with a retry button + \"check your connection\" hint."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0568d5f9d03337ce",
      "line_num": 704,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bug"
      ],
      "text": "**Wizard `whisper` status stuck at `pending` after a whisper Sprig\u2122 graft (cosmetic \u2014 STT works): On the slim rootstock, grafting `whisper-base-ggml` serves STT correctly (`/api/v1/audio/transcriptions` \u2192 200 through the sprig), but `GET /api/v1/retrieval/models/status` keeps `models.whisper != \"ready\"`. `point_stt_at` ([sprigs/stt_dispatch.py:40](app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/stt_dispatch.py#L40)) flips it \"ready\" and IS called by the graft route ([routers/sprigs.py:113](app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/sprigs.py#L113)); single worker (`UVICORN_WORKERS=1`), the status dict is seeded with a `whisper` key ([main.py:1119](app/backend/sage_is_ai/main.py#L1119)), and the endpoint returns that dict raw ([retrieval.py:439](app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/retrieval.py#L439)) \u2014 so static analysis says it SHOULD read ready. Needs LIVE inspection: `KEEP=1 make sprig_smoke`, then `curl \u2026/models/status` right after the whisper graft, plus a temp log in `point_stt_at`. Surfaced 2026-07-22 by `sprig_smoke` (which now self-heals auth + dumps the real status object on failure). Low severity \u2014 this is the wizard's HF-download-skippable optimization, not the STT capability itself.**\n- [ ] Surface HF download progress (bytes, last-byte timestamp) to `request.app.state.MODEL_DOWNLOAD_STATUS` so the status endpoint exposes liveness.\n- [ ] Watchdog in `_download` (`retrieval.py`): if cache size hasn't grown in N minutes (configurable, default 5), mark status=`stalled`, capture the error, allow retry via a re-POST to `/api/v1/retrieval/models/download`.\n- [ ] Surface stalled state in the wizard UI with a retry button + \"check your connection\" hint.\n\n`#bug`\n`TODO.md:L704`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1060,
      "y": 960
    },
