        "#critical"
      ],
      "text": "**Phase 1 \u2014 ui-Sprig contract + cookie bridge SHIPPED (2026-07-27; all gates green \u2014 full `make e2e` 40 tests/39 passing/1 pending-by-design, `make sprig_smoke` 66/66, new `make ui_sprig_gate` 22/22; committed). The cookie bridge came in far under estimate. The plan treats it as a prerequisite subsystem (\"server-rendered pages can't read localStorage, where the login token lives (478 references)\"), but `utils/auth.get_current_user` ALREADY falls back to the `token` cookie and every sign-in path already sets it httponly \u2014 so a render route had identity for free. What was actually missing is that a PAGE must fail differently from an API: `pages/auth.py` is ~30 lines that call the existing function and turn its refusal into a 307 to `/auth?next=<path>` (path only, never an absolute URL \u2014 a redirect target echoed from a request is an open redirect), with a signed-in non-admin getting 403 instead, because bouncing them to sign-in would loop. A near-miss worth recording: the first draft of that file decoded the JWT and looked the user up itself, and within twenty lines had drifted from the original in two directions \u2014 it added a session check the API path does not make, and it MISSED the try.sage session-reset cutoff the API path does, so a facilitator pressing \"Reset now\" would have invalidated every API session while leaving these pages signed in. Caught before it ran. The audit behind the plan counted authorization restated ~142 times in the frontend; a second copy in the BACKEND is the same disease with a shorter incubation. Guard-rail `pages-cookie-auth.cy.ts` 4/4, including the non-admin branch (creates and deletes its own user, since the shared-container guard watches admin config and not the user table) and an assertion that the cookie is httpOnly \u2014 the property that makes this an improvement on localStorage rather than a second copy of the same risk. The ui-Sprig contract: `sprigs/ui_dispatch.py` + a graft branch mirroring `theme` exactly, `SPRIG_ACTIVE_UI` / `SPRIG_UI_SCRIPTING_GRANT` persisted pointers, `/ui/active.html` serving the active fragment, a server-rendered slot in the page shell, and `POST /sprigs/ui/scripting` for the grant. First-party example `scripts/ui-sprigs/workshop-welcome/` + `scripts/build-sprig-ui.sh` (dockerized oras, sanity gate mirroring the validator but STRICTER \u2014 script is refused at build unconditionally, so an author has to think hard before publishing a fragment that only works if every operator grants it something), published to the local registry and pinned in the catalog. Lifecycle proven end to end against the real artifact (`ui-sprig-contract.cy.ts` 5/5): graft \u2192 serve \u2192 render into the slot \u2192 prune \u2192 pointer cleared \u2192 grant revoked. The grant is held by NAME, not as a boolean \u2014 a decision made about a fragment an admin read cannot be inherited by whatever is grafted next, and `/ui/active.html` plus the slot both REVALIDATE before serving, so revoking takes effect without a regraft. \"No framework sprigs\" is enforced three ways, and the memo is honest that no regex decides it: scripting off unless granted; when granted, inline only (so what runs is what was reviewed) and same-origin (so no CDN can supply one); and a 16 kB script ceiling \u2014 sized from measurement, since the runtime probe showed an independently-built Svelte biome inlines ~18 kB minimum, which makes bundling one structurally impossible while leaving an island room. New gate `make ui_sprig_gate` (`scripts/smoke/ui-sprig-validator.sh`, in `gauntlet_full`) proves 22 REFUSALS against the real validator in the real image \u2014 asserting the REASON, not just the outcome, because a validator that refuses everything for the wrong cause passes a refusal test while telling operators nothing. Gate-quality note: that script passed silently on its first run because `docker run` without `-i` gave python an empty stdin \u2014 it checked nothing and exited 0. Fixed with `-i` plus a sentinel that fails unless the run prints a verdict line; a gate whose silence is indistinguishable from success is worse than no gate. Deliberately NOT done: the `app.html` slot for the SPA (the slot lives in the page shell, the surface that survives the migration; adding one to the bundle we are deleting would mean injecting fragment markup into SvelteKit for a stack with a known end date).**\n- [ ] Write \"no framework sprigs\" into the ui-Sprig spec repo \u2014 fragments + host runtime only; carry the THREE mechanisms above (grant, inline-and-same-origin, size ceiling) and the honest note that no static check decides \"is this a framework\" #sprigs #ui\n\n`#sprigs` `#ui` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L446`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 690
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "d5e1753921cf30dd",
      "line_num": 449,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#critical"
      ],
      "text": "**Migration test discipline made mechanical \u2014 surface registry, dual-target runner, parity gate (Alexander, 2026-07-28: \"we don't have always enough Cypress tests before we cut into things\u2026 they would have caught these issues\", and specs \"should work against both the original Svelte and the new build by just being pointed at them\"). Both halves were right, and the first one is the sharper criticism: writing the spec first is NOT enough when the person writing it also chooses what it covers. The diagnostics guard-rail was written against Svelte first, passed against both implementations, and the no-build page was still missing most of the old one \u2014 plus a \"Show me how to fix this\" button that rendered and did nothing. Every assertion passed because I had written assertions for the parts I had built. Three devices. (1) `app/cypress/support/surfaces.ts` \u2014 one registry mapping a surface NAME to its two paths, replacing the per-spec env vars the first two specs each invented (`CYPRESS_SPRIGS_PANEL`, `CYPRESS_DIAGNOSTICS_PANEL`); a spec now asks for `surfacePath('sprigs')` and the runner decides which implementation it gets. Registering a surface is the FIRST step of migrating it, before code moves, because that is when \"what does the old one actually do?\" still has to be answered. (2) `make e2e_both` runs the whole suite once per target, so \"green against both\" is what the gate means rather than what somebody remembered to check \u2014 wired into `gauntlet_full` in place of the single-target `e2e`. (3) `surface-parity.cy.ts` \u2014 the check that does not depend on my judgement. It asserts nothing about behaviour: it visits both implementations in one run, collects the `data-cy` hooks each RENDERS, and fails if the no-build page is missing any the legacy page offers. It cannot be satisfied by writing a narrower spec, because it never reads the spec. Its contract is that every interactive control on a legacy surface carries a `data-cy`, added before migration \u2014 enumerating the old surface's controls is exactly the step that gets skipped. It went RED on first run and named the work: `diag-command-library, diag-ghost-endpoints, diag-reprobe, diag-technical`. (Its first failure message said only \"expected [ Array(4) ] to deeply equal []\" \u2014 a gate that says something is missing without saying what is half a gate, so it asserts on a joined string and the failure carries the work list.) All four then built, taking diagnostics from ~40% of the old surface to parity: technical detail and ghost endpoints as native `<details>` (the plan's \"native HTML over custom widgets\" \u2014 they replace the Svelte `Collapsible` outright), per-row re-probe posting to a fragment route that calls the API handler rather than reimplementing it (that handler refuses any URL not currently configured \u2014 rule R6 \u2014 and routing around it to save lines would route around the SSRF defence), and the recovery-command library. `commandLibrary.ts` joined `fixRegistry.ts` in being extracted to JSON both sides read; the extraction script silently produced an EMPTY array first time by anchoring on the `[` in `CommandEntry[]` rather than the assignment, so it now refuses to write nothing. Honest limit written into the parity spec: identical hooks are a coverage FLOOR, not proof of equivalence \u2014 a button that renders and does nothing has the same hook as one that works. Behaviour stays each surface spec's job. Gates: `gauntlet_full` exit 0, both targets green (legacy 55 tests/52 passing/3 nobuild-only pending, nobuild 55/55).**\n`#frontend` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L449`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 780
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f7c29b908340fa15",
      "line_num": 49,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**try.sage Manual Regression Sign-off (2026-04-27): Phase A backend + Phase B frontend shipped. All 12 smoke checks passed (container boot, persona magic-links, banner, switcher, tutorial, admin tab, hidden connection, model filter, env-gate disable). Reference left for future regression-pass authors.**\n`TODO.md:L49`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 870
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0a97b30b37b22d9a",
      "line_num": 106,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#security"
      ],
      "text": "**Node lockfile CVE sweep \u2014 17 \u2192 0 (2026-07-26): the `audit-deps` pre-commit hook (trivy, `HIGH,CRITICAL`, fires only on `bun.lock`/`requirements.txt`) had not run in a while because nothing touched `bun.lock`. The Phase Q cruft sweep touched it and woke the hook: 17 findings (15 HIGH, 2 CRITICAL). Pre-existing, not sweep-caused \u2014 the sweep's lockfile diff is 13 lines, all deletions, and scanning `HEAD`'s untouched `bun.lock` returns the identical 17. All were transitive; fixed by raising the existing `overrides` floors plus one direct bump, then regenerating in Docker (`make bun_install`): `tar >=7.5.11 \u2192 >=7.5.19` (CRIT gzip bomb), new `protobufjs >=7.6.1` (CRIT RCE via injected type fields, from `onnxruntime-web`), new `linkify-it >=5.0.2` (from `markdown-it`, the one that genuinely ships to the browser), new `brace-expansion >=5.0.8` (from `minimatch`), new `ws >=8.21.0` (from `engine.io-client`/`pyodide`), new `sharp >=0.35.0` (from `@huggingface/transformers`; resolved 0.35.3, the one major bump, and it installed clean), and direct `undici ^7.24.0 \u2192 ^7.28.0` (a pin only \u2014 0 `src/` imports, pulled by `cheerio` \u2190 the dev-only `i18next-parser`). Exposure context: `node_modules` is NOT baked into the runtime image (the Dockerfile copies only `/app/build/`), so apart from what Vite bundles this was build-time surface, not shipped-runtime surface. Verified: `trivy` 0 vulnerabilities, exit 0; `make it_build` frozen-lockfile build green; `make e2e` 11/13 with only the two known pre-existing leak failures below. We could NOT delete our way out of the two biggest \u2014 `@huggingface/transformers` is live in `Leaderboard.svelte` and `kokoro.worker.ts`, and pulls `onnxruntime-web` with it. Committed.**\n`#security`\n`TODO.md:L106`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 960
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 147,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "**4 outlier recipes stay OFF the lib** (by design \u2014 none has the standard arch/push blocks; forcing them = over-fit): `build-sprig-theme.sh` (arch-neutral 846-byte token artifacts), `build-sprig-minilm.sh` (arch-neutral ONNX weights, custom push \u2014 **also carries the macOS `bsdtar` lacks-`--sort=name` reproducibility bug: switch its pack to GNU-tar-in-docker when next touched**), `build-sprig-reranker.sh` (GGUF, two push paths), `repack-sprig-arch.sh` (re-tags an existing artifact \u2014 different tool). Optional light reuse: they COULD call `sprig_build_defaults` + `sprig_ensure_registry` for constants/registry only."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 148,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "**`wizard-smoke.sh` keeps its own helper style** (`require`/`fail` with container-log dump, `KEEP_ON_FAIL`) \u2014 could adopt `gate.sh` for consistency, but its fail-shape differs; low priority."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 149,
          "tags": [
            "#bonsai"
          ],
          "text": "**`parity_gate` EXERCISED for the first time \u2014 green** (2026-07-27). Context: the gate SKIPs rather than FAILs when its 8.I.3 artifacts are absent (shipped 2026-07-22, so `gauntlet_full` does not halt on a not-yet-built gate) \u2014 but it turned out to be **structurally unrunnable**, not merely unbuilt: `harness.py` READS `/w/harness/reference.json`, and nothing in the repo ever wrote it, so no invocation of `make parity_gate` could ever have exercised parity. Two missing halves now shipped: **`scripts/gates/embedding-parity/gen-reference.py`** (sentence-transformers embeddings + HF tokenizer ids, emitted in the exact order `harness.py` expects \u2014 `parity + corpus + queries` \u2014 and merged per-model so several cultivars can share the file) and **`scripts/gates/embedding-parity/build-artifacts.sh`** (one container: cpu-torch, HF pull, `convert_hf_to_gguf.py` at the SAME pinned `b9859` as `build-llama-static.sh`, `llama-quantize` to Q8_0, then the reference). Egress IS required at build time; nothing produced here ships in the image, so the runtime zero-egress property is untouched. **FIRST REAL RESULT \u2014 both quants pass with room:** `e5-f16` cos_mean=1.00000 **cos_min=0.99999** tok_mismatch=0 recall=5/5 top3agree=15/15 (needs \u22650.999); `e5-q8` cos_mean=0.99966 **cos_min=0.99913** tok_mismatch=0 recall=5/5 top3agree=15/15 (needs \u22650.99 + full recall). `tok_mismatch=0` on both means the Korean/Hangul canary that held minilm and bge back is clean for e5 \u2014 which is why e5 is the shipped cultivar. Verified the static musl `llama-quantize` runs under Debian glibc (the any-libc property holds). Two traps burned in for the next runner: bind-mounts must NOT nest inside the `$OUT` mount (Docker Desktop virtiofs refuses it \u2014 `$BIN` already lives inside `$OUT`, so mount the gate scripts on their own path), and a bare `snapshot_download` pulls safetensors AND pytorch-bin AND onnx AND openvino (**9.0GB measured**), so the script now ignores the redundant formats (~2.3GB; the kept/dropped split was verified against the live repo file list, not assumed). Artifacts live at `/tmp/sprig-build/8i3/{bin,gguf,harness}` and are NOT in git \u2014 rerun `build-artifacts.sh` after a machine wipe or a llama.cpp tag bump."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "41e7197f891c285a",
      "line_num": 146,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Shared build/gate libraries \u2014 DRY pass (2026-07-22; committed): extracted two shared libs to kill copy-pasted boilerplate. `scripts/lib/gate.sh` \u2014 PASS/FAIL + `ok`/`no`/`require`/`gate_summary`; adopted by `verify-image-manifest.sh` + `manifest-verify-fixture.sh` + the 4 heavy smoke gates (lifecycle/upgrade/durability/signing), all re-certified green via `gauntlet_full` (66/66, 12/12, 10/10). `scripts/lib/sprig-build.sh` \u2014 `sprig_build_defaults` (the 8 OCI/registry constants), `sprig_arch_normalize`, portable `sha256`, `sprig_ensure_registry`, `sprig_push` (optional `SIG_LAYER` superset), `sprig_timing_start|end`. 9 `build-sprig-*` recipes now source it (tika, docling, backup-rclone, rag-loaders, export-document, media-ffmpeg, dev-svelte, vector-chroma, whisper) \u2014 ~270 lines of duplication gone; every recipe gained the \u23f1 footer. Certified by REAL rebuild across 3 shapes: tika (jlink server, both arches \u2014 sha256 byte-identical to the CATALOG pins), backup-rclone (static-download deliver + SIGN), rag-loaders (pip). Hazards caught during rollout: set-e safety (helpers `return 0` past benign falses \u2014 arm64 arch, registry poll); `TAG=v2` preserved for dev-svelte/vector-chroma; whisper's out-of-block `ORAS_IMG`/`sha256` de-duplicated.**\n- [ ] **4 outlier recipes stay OFF the lib** (by design \u2014 none has the standard arch/push blocks; forcing them = over-fit): `build-sprig-theme.sh` (arch-neutral 846-byte token artifacts), `build-sprig-minilm.sh` (arch-neutral ONNX weights, custom push \u2014 **also carries the macOS `bsdtar` lacks-`--sort=name` reproducibility bug: switch its pack to GNU-tar-in-docker when next touched**), `build-sprig-reranker.sh` (GGUF, two push paths), `repack-sprig-arch.sh` (re-tags an existing artifact \u2014 different tool). Optional light reuse: they COULD call `sprig_build_defaults` + `sprig_ensure_registry` for constants/registry only.\n- [ ] **`wizard-smoke.sh` keeps its own helper style** (`require`/`fail` with container-log dump, `KEEP_ON_FAIL`) \u2014 could adopt `gate.sh` for consistency, but its fail-shape differs; low priority.\n- [x] **`parity_gate` EXERCISED for the first time \u2014 green** (2026-07-27). Context: the gate SKIPs rather than FAILs when its 8.I.3 artifacts are absent (shipped 2026-07-22, so `gauntlet_full` does not halt on a not-yet-built gate) \u2014 but it turned out to be **structurally unrunnable**, not merely unbuilt: `harness.py` READS `/w/harness/reference.json`, and nothing in the repo ever wrote it, so no invocation of `make parity_gate` could ever have exercised parity. Two missing halves now shipped: **`scripts/gates/embedding-parity/gen-reference.py`** (sentence-transformers embeddings + HF tokenizer ids, emitted in the exact order `harness.py` expects \u2014 `parity + corpus + queries` \u2014 and merged per-model so several cultivars can share the file) and **`scripts/gates/embedding-parity/build-artifacts.sh`** (one container: cpu-torch, HF pull, `convert_hf_to_gguf.py` at the SAME pinned `b9859` as `build-llama-static.sh`, `llama-quantize` to Q8_0, then the reference). Egress IS required at build time; nothing produced here ships in the image, so the runtime zero-egress property is untouched. **FIRST REAL RESULT \u2014 both quants pass with room:** `e5-f16` cos_mean=1.00000 **cos_min=0.99999** tok_mismatch=0 recall=5/5 top3agree=15/15 (needs \u22650.999); `e5-q8` cos_mean=0.99966 **cos_min=0.99913** tok_mismatch=0 recall=5/5 top3agree=15/15 (needs \u22650.99 + full recall). `tok_mismatch=0` on both means the Korean/Hangul canary that held minilm and bge back is clean for e5 \u2014 which is why e5 is the shipped cultivar. Verified the static musl `llama-quantize` runs under Debian glibc (the any-libc property holds). Two traps burned in for the next runner: bind-mounts must NOT nest inside the `$OUT` mount (Docker Desktop virtiofs refuses it \u2014 `$BIN` already lives inside `$OUT`, so mount the gate scripts on their own path), and a bare `snapshot_download` pulls safetensors AND pytorch-bin AND onnx AND openvino (**9.0GB measured**), so the script now ignores the redundant formats (~2.3GB; the kept/dropped split was verified against the live repo file list, not assumed). Artifacts live at `/tmp/sprig-build/8i3/{bin,gguf,harness}` and are NOT in git \u2014 rerun `build-artifacts.sh` after a machine wipe or a llama.cpp tag bump. #bonsai\n\n`TODO.md:L146`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
