      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "78e0bb039745c7f8",
      "line_num": 264,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bonsai",
        "#strategic-decision"
      ],
      "text": "**Confirm Bonsai\u2122 metaphor horizon before `bonsai.sage.is` ships (2026-06-28; DECIDED 2026-07-08): Rich Harris's strategic question answered by Alexander \u2014 Bonsai\u2122 is sticking as the 10-year name, so `bonsai.sage.is` ships as the canonical spec URL and the hub scaffold proceeds against it. The `spec.sage.is` reservation above stays as the escape hatch per Daniel Stenberg's guardrail (parked DNS, no content).**\n- [x] Founders' decision: is Bonsai\u2122 the long-horizon name? \u2014 YES (Alexander, 2026-07-08)\n- [x] `bonsai.sage.is` ships as canonical (hub scaffold built the same day)\n- [ ] Document the decision and the reasoning in a brief `docs/` note so future-us doesn't relitigate\n\n`#bonsai` `#strategic-decision`\n`TODO.md:L264`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 1860
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 438,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#biomes",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Spike-prove the shared-runtime assumption \u2014 HOLDS** (2026-07-27, `tools/spikes/biomes/`, memo at `MEMO.md`, `run.sh` re-runs it). The plan makes biomes load-bearing for the Phase 3 shell flip and explicitly refuses to assume the part that matters; Phase S said nothing about it, because vanilla streaming needs no runtime at all. **16/16 checks across three build shapes**, four of them against a shape that MUST fail \u2014 a suite that has never said no is not evidence (the Phase S autoscroll went 13/13 green and was broken in the hand). **Shape A** (one Vite build, three biomes \u2014 the Phase 3 flip): one runtime, one shared-store instance, a store written in biome A observed in biome B, all three mounted from plain HTML with no SvelteKit. Identity, not resemblance \u2014 each biome hands the page its own reference to Svelte's scheduler and the page compares them. **Shape B** (three independent builds, the CONTROL): three runtimes, no shared scheduler, no shared store, and the probe detects all of it. **Shape C** (a biome built LATER, borrowing the host runtime by URL \u2014 the ui-Sprig and post-flip-migration case): 1.6 kB, shares both. **Three costs the plan had not priced.** (1) *\"Externalise the runtime\" is not one specifier* \u2014 marking `svelte/internal` external and leaving `svelte` alone lets the package entry reach the same sources under a different module id, so the late biome inlined a second copy of the lifecycle code with its own `current_component`; the build SUCCEEDED and the biome threw `Function called outside component initialization` at mount. Twenty minutes here; in a marketplace it fails in someone else's deployment after graft. (2) *Publishing the runtime defeats tree-shaking* \u2014 a stable public interface means exporting all of it, 27.3 kB \u2192 95.4 kB unminified (3.5\u00d7). Phase 3 does not pay this (all biomes build together); the Sprig marketplace does, and each late biome then costs 1.6 kB and nothing more, so it is a fixed premium against a per-extension saving. (3) *Vite mangles entry exports by default* \u2014 `preserveEntrySignatures: false` shipped the runtime as `flush as j`, so the late biome asked for the real names, got a resolution error and never mounted. All three are one shape: the host must publish a deliberate interface and every default is against it. **Observed, not asserted:** a biome is a CSS boundary (an app rule does not reach inside a shadowed biome; `shadow: 'none'` removes the boundary in one line and the encapsulation with it \u2014 both measured, every surface must pick); and the SvelteKit surface is **64 of 317 components, nine symbols**, with `goto` (54 sites) + `page` (26) = 80 of 81, so ~50 lines of shim covers it and a biome importing navigation/stores/environment builds and runs, `$page` reacting to the shim's own navigation. **What it does NOT say:** it never put a biome on a page beside the LEGACY SvelteKit bundle (phases 2\u20133 have both, two runtimes by construction; the flip removes the SPA router so the overlap is bounded, but nobody has built that page); and these biomes are toys \u2014 the chat core's 710 click handlers and 859 bindings are untested as a custom element. Phase 4 unblocked on runtime grounds."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 439,
          "tags": [
            "#sprigs",
            "#ui"
          ],
          "text": "Write costs (1) and (2) above into the ui-Sprig spec \u2014 they are the MECHANISM behind \"no framework sprigs\", not a matter of taste"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 440,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#bonsai"
          ],
          "text": "**Version `/themes/active.css` once the shell is server-rendered** (deferred here from the 2026-07-26 caching pass, because it needs shell templating this phase delivers for free). MEASURED on the shipped image with no theme grafted \u2014 the common case: `HTTP 200`, `cache-control: no-cache`, **no ETag, no Last-Modified**, and a conditional request with `If-None-Match` still returns **200, not 304**. So every full page load spends a render-blocking round-trip on 23 bytes of `/* no theme grafted */`, via the static `<link>` at `app.html:21`. Do NOT invalidate this on image/container updates: `SPRIG_ACTIVE_THEME` is flipped by a graft and cleared by a prune at RUNTIME (`routers/sprigs.py:151,305`) with no restart, so deploy-keyed invalidation would leave an operator's freshly grafted theme invisible until restart \u2014 the exact failure the Sprig durability work exists to prevent. Right design: inject `?v=<active theme name + css mtime>` into the shell's `<link>` at serve time, then serve the CSS `immutable, max-age=31536000`. A graft changes the token, so the URL changes, so pickup is instant AND there is zero revalidation in between (same trick SvelteKit uses for `_app/immutable`). Interim options considered and rejected for now: a short TTL (a grafted theme could appear one load late) and an ETag-only fix (saves a 23-byte body, not the round-trip)."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0b40bc75f413b8ae",
      "line_num": 437,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**Phase S \u2014 streaming spike: GO, with two conditions (2026-07-27, `tools/spikes/streaming/`, memo at `MEMO.md`). All four done-criteria measured, not asserted: HTTP/2 (`http_version 2` on shell, island and vendored htmx, via Caddy `flush_interval -1` \u2014 without that line the proxy buffers and the spike would have looked fine while proving nothing); incremental delivery (frame 1 at 0.000s \u2192 frame 150 at 3.337s); stop really stops \u2014 client `{outcome:\"stopped\", tokens:219/453}` AND server `stream_aborted sent=21 total=164`, which is the half that matters, since a stop button that only hides output is theatre; autoscroll holds (pinned at 0px, releases on a real wheel gesture and stays). Browser criteria 8/8 under Playwright, TTFT 5ms. ~260 lines of stdlib server + framework-free island did what the SvelteKit path does. Two findings that change the plan. (1) Cypress cannot see streaming \u2014 it MITM-proxies the AUT and buffers the body, so the page observes nothing until the stream ends (its stop test failed `aborted-before-open`: the `fetch` had not resolved). Not fixable with better assertions; any streaming guard-rail needs a non-proxying driver. Phase 0's \"specs pass against old and new\" holds for DOM surfaces, NOT streaming ones. (2) Autoscroll took FOUR attempts, and attempt 3 passed every automated check while being broken on real hardware \u2014 the most valuable thing this spike produced. (a) `scroll` + distance-from-bottom drags the reader back (`scrollTop 135\u2192159`, flag still \"pinned\") because `scroll` is async. (b) Remembering the position we scrolled TO fails too: a `scroll` event carries no position, and our own scroll has overwritten it by delivery time. (c) Inferring intent from gesture deltas (`wheel` up \u21d2 release) went 13/13 green under Playwright and failed in the hand \u2014 a trackpad emits tiny negative deltas from the slightest contact, so the pin released on PAGE LOAD before the view moved; and `touchmove` fires early in a swipe while still near the bottom, so touch measured \"pinned\" and momentum carried the reader away with nothing left to re-evaluate. Both reported by Alexander testing manually, neither by the suite. (d) Shipped: judge by POSITION on every scroll event, plus a rule refusing to yank a reader who has moved the box (closing (a)'s race). No gesture listeners at all \u2014 momentum, scrollbar drag and keyboard paging all come free. Manually confirmed working. Two further bugs fell out and WERE caught by the suite: a smooth jump-scroll released the pin mid-animation (intermediate events sit far from the bottom, so it is instant now), and the jump button rendered on load because startr.style's `display: var(--d) !important` defeats the `hidden` attribute \u2014 a framework-level footgun worth an upstream `[hidden]{display:none!important}` rule, NOT edited (Alexander's repo, every-consumer blast radius). Svelte gives you nothing on autoscroll either \u2014 same ~30 lines \u2014 so the cost is a wash, but the DISCOVERY cost was not. GO is conditional: a non-proxying driver lands before any streaming surface is cut \u2014 NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT, since attempt (c) above was 13/13 green under exactly such a driver and still broken on real hardware, so every streamed surface needs a human pass before it is called done (a scheduling fact for Phases 2\u20134, not a nicety); and Phase 4 stays gated on the shared-runtime probe below \u2014 this spike tested vanilla streaming, which needs no runtime at all, so nothing here licenses it. Recommendation: proceed to Phase 0 (a non-streaming surface) while both questions settle in parallel.**\n- [x] **Spike-prove the shared-runtime assumption \u2014 HOLDS** (2026-07-27, `tools/spikes/biomes/`, memo at `MEMO.md`, `run.sh` re-runs it). The plan makes biomes load-bearing for the Phase 3 shell flip and explicitly refuses to assume the part that matters; Phase S said nothing about it, because vanilla streaming needs no runtime at all. **16/16 checks across three build shapes**, four of them against a shape that MUST fail \u2014 a suite that has never said no is not evidence (the Phase S autoscroll went 13/13 green and was broken in the hand). **Shape A** (one Vite build, three biomes \u2014 the Phase 3 flip): one runtime, one shared-store instance, a store written in biome A observed in biome B, all three mounted from plain HTML with no SvelteKit. Identity, not resemblance \u2014 each biome hands the page its own reference to Svelte's scheduler and the page compares them. **Shape B** (three independent builds, the CONTROL): three runtimes, no shared scheduler, no shared store, and the probe detects all of it. **Shape C** (a biome built LATER, borrowing the host runtime by URL \u2014 the ui-Sprig and post-flip-migration case): 1.6 kB, shares both. **Three costs the plan had not priced.** (1) *\"Externalise the runtime\" is not one specifier* \u2014 marking `svelte/internal` external and leaving `svelte` alone lets the package entry reach the same sources under a different module id, so the late biome inlined a second copy of the lifecycle code with its own `current_component`; the build SUCCEEDED and the biome threw `Function called outside component initialization` at mount. Twenty minutes here; in a marketplace it fails in someone else's deployment after graft. (2) *Publishing the runtime defeats tree-shaking* \u2014 a stable public interface means exporting all of it, 27.3 kB \u2192 95.4 kB unminified (3.5\u00d7). Phase 3 does not pay this (all biomes build together); the Sprig marketplace does, and each late biome then costs 1.6 kB and nothing more, so it is a fixed premium against a per-extension saving. (3) *Vite mangles entry exports by default* \u2014 `preserveEntrySignatures: false` shipped the runtime as `flush as j`, so the late biome asked for the real names, got a resolution error and never mounted. All three are one shape: the host must publish a deliberate interface and every default is against it. **Observed, not asserted:** a biome is a CSS boundary (an app rule does not reach inside a shadowed biome; `shadow: 'none'` removes the boundary in one line and the encapsulation with it \u2014 both measured, every surface must pick); and the SvelteKit surface is **64 of 317 components, nine symbols**, with `goto` (54 sites) + `page` (26) = 80 of 81, so ~50 lines of shim covers it and a biome importing navigation/stores/environment builds and runs, `$page` reacting to the shim's own navigation. **What it does NOT say:** it never put a biome on a page beside the LEGACY SvelteKit bundle (phases 2\u20133 have both, two runtimes by construction; the flip removes the SPA router so the overlap is bounded, but nobody has built that page); and these biomes are toys \u2014 the chat core's 710 click handlers and 859 bindings are untested as a custom element. Phase 4 unblocked on runtime grounds. #frontend #biomes #critical\n- [ ] Write costs (1) and (2) above into the ui-Sprig spec \u2014 they are the MECHANISM behind \"no framework sprigs\", not a matter of taste #sprigs #ui\n- [ ] **Version `/themes/active.css` once the shell is server-rendered** (deferred here from the 2026-07-26 caching pass, because it needs shell templating this phase delivers for free). MEASURED on the shipped image with no theme grafted \u2014 the common case: `HTTP 200`, `cache-control: no-cache`, **no ETag, no Last-Modified**, and a conditional request with `If-None-Match` still returns **200, not 304**. So every full page load spends a render-blocking round-trip on 23 bytes of `/* no theme grafted */`, via the static `<link>` at `app.html:21`. Do NOT invalidate this on image/container updates: `SPRIG_ACTIVE_THEME` is flipped by a graft and cleared by a prune at RUNTIME (`routers/sprigs.py:151,305`) with no restart, so deploy-keyed invalidation would leave an operator's freshly grafted theme invisible until restart \u2014 the exact failure the Sprig durability work exists to prevent. Right design: inject `?v=<active theme name + css mtime>` into the shell's `<link>` at serve time, then serve the CSS `immutable, max-age=31536000`. A graft changes the token, so the URL changes, so pickup is instant AND there is zero revalidation in between (same trick SvelteKit uses for `_app/immutable`). Interim options considered and rejected for now: a short TTL (a grafted theme could appear one load late) and an ETag-only fix (saves a 23-byte body, not the round-trip). #perf #bonsai\n\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L437`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 1950
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "58acdb2b44a89bbe",
      "line_num": 451,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**startr.style adopted on the no-build pages (unversioned CDN, by decision) (Alexander, 2026-07-28: \"the unversioned for now, we'll be updating to versioned soon\"). Loaded before `pages.css` so local rules win. The split is the point, and it is enforced rather than intended: startr.style props carry one-off layout on markup we AUTHOR (the shell, headings, spacing \u2014 where inline props read well), `pages.css` carries anything REPEATED across generated rows, anything the prop vocabulary cannot express (keyframes, `:focus-visible`, media queries on generated markup), and anything that must survive the CDN being down. Generated card/row markup deliberately keeps classes: a Python f-string emitting forty characters of `style` per row reads and diffs worse than a class. Verified in a real browser that the props actually apply (`--maxw` \u2192 780px, `--p-md` \u2192 22.5px, `--lh`, `--size` all live). New gate `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts`: with startr.style genuinely blocked, both pages still render every control, rows keep `display:grid` from the local sheet, and there is no horizontal overflow \u2014 because an unversioned URL is a single point of failure by construction and an air-gapped Rootstock is that case permanently. Two process notes worth more than the feature. (1) My first outage \"proof\" used a Playwright glob (`startr.style/`) that matched NOTHING \u2014 zero requests blocked \u2014 and passed while measuring the online page; caught only by printing the blocked-request count. The spec uses a regex and warns the next editor, since an aborted `<link>` still appears in `document.styleSheets` with its href, making \"is it loaded\" a useless signal. (2) The new gate was then proved able to FAIL, by running a deliberately-wrong assertion that the framework HAD applied and confirming it went red. EGRESS, recorded in `shell.py` rather than discovered later: this is the one place the product reaches off-machine on a normal page load, against a Rootstock that in-houses Sprigs to avoid HuggingFace pulls and refuses theme/ui bundles that reference external URLs. A stylesheet leaks far less than a script \u2014 no cross-origin cookies, cannot read the page \u2014 but it does tell startr.style that someone opened an admin page, and an air-gapped deployment gets unstyled chrome. Both stop being true when the versioned URL lands with SRI, or when a self-host flag serves a vendored copy. NOT applied to ui-Sprig fragments: the validator refuses external references and that contract stands. Gates: `gauntlet_full` exit 0, both targets green (legacy 57/52 + 5 nobuild-only pending, nobuild 57/57).**\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L451`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2040
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "fd2fceb95769f12c",
      "line_num": 453,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**The migration convention is written down (2026-07-28, `docs/no-build-surface-convention.md`). Two surfaces through the process \u2014 Sprigs 231 \u2192 157 (32%) and Diagnostics 813 \u2192 273 (67%, at parity, verified by the parity gate rather than claimed) \u2014 is enough evidence to stop re-deciding it per surface. The doc carries the ORDER of work (register \u2192 hook the legacy controls \u2192 guard-rail green on legacy \u2192 build \u2192 let the parity gate find what you missed \u2192 `e2e_both` \u2192 measure), and every design rule with the surface that paid for it. The rule that explains most of the gap between 32% and 67%: native HTML before custom widgets \u2014 `<details>` deleted the Svelte `Collapsible` and the 267-line how-to-fix modal outright. Also recorded as standing limits rather than footnotes: the parity gate is a coverage FLOOR (a button that renders and does nothing has the same hook as one that works), and a green suite is the weakest evidence on an interactive surface, so every surface gets a human pass via `scripts/manual-check.sh` before it takes over a route.**\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L453`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2130
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "d2646a6e73fa321f",
      "line_num": 455,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#bonsai"
      ],
      "text": "**Graft errors persist on the Sprig; panel messages became CSS toasts (Alexander, 2026-07-28: errors \"should also stay as an additional inline to the sprig that hit them\u2026 they stay visible even on page reload\", and the messages \"can be fixed to act as toasts by using a css animation to fade them out after a little bit also gluing it to the top of the window\"). The error belongs to the Sprig, not to the session or the clicker. `supervisor.record_error(name, message, phase)` writes into `state.json` beside `grafted` on the DATA VOLUME, so it outlives a reload, a restart, and a full container recreation \u2014 verified against a real recreation rather than asserted, since a durability claim tested only by reloading the tab is not a durability test. It is cleared by a successful graft or a successful prune and by nothing else, because a restart does not fix a missing dependency and so must not look like it did. Scope was Alexander's call \u2014 \"property of the sprig as it affects all the team using the Sage.is AI-UI instance\" \u2014 so `/catalog` carries `errors` and every operator sees the same record. The toast is the notification; the card is the record. Panel messages are now a CSS animation fixed to the top of the viewport, 6s for a success and 20s for an error, because an error names a cause you were not expecting and six seconds is not long enough to read one. Fixed positioning is the actual fix to what Alexander hit: the message rendered inside the panel, and on a long list it appeared off-screen. No script is involved \u2014 htmx swaps the WHOLE panel, so every message is a new element and the animation restarts by itself, which is the one place \"swap the whole panel\" pays a dividend nobody designed for.**\n`#frontend` `#bonsai`\n`TODO.md:L455`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2220
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "20835818cb1bd1bd",
      "line_num": 465,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**All twelve panels converted to Jinja2 (2026-08-01; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 163 tests/131 passing/32 pending, nobuild 163/163 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). The spike's recommendation, carried out. Not one guard-rail spec was touched \u2014 that was the contract and it held for all twelve.**\n`TODO.md:L465`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2310
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "e2148a5688456aca",
      "line_num": 479,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Templating spike \u2014 Jinja2 on one panel, and the dev-loop claim holds (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 163 tests/131 passing/32 pending, nobuild 163/163 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Templating was rejected once on line count and reopened on Alexander's _\"We may lower this bar. as engines often come with other benefits.\"_ One panel converted, `welcome_panel.py`, so the decision on the other eleven rests on numbers.**\n`TODO.md:L479`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2400
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "932d22e720515e49",
      "line_num": 502,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**`/pages/` is a route index now, not the chat window (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 163 tests/131 passing/32 pending, nobuild 163/163 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Asked for as a dev affordance; built always-on and admin-only instead, on the argument that an index existing only in a review container is one you cannot open on the instance you are actually debugging \u2014 and nothing is disclosed by it, since an admin can already reach all thirteen pages and until now had to know the URLs. It also closes a real confusion: `/pages/` used to answer with the SPA shell, because the app is mounted at `/` with an index.html fallback, so asking for a pages route silently gave you the chat window. Registered as BOTH `\"\"` and `\"/\"` rather than leaning on redirect-on-missing-slash, for exactly that reason \u2014 an unclaimed path here is answered by the app rather than redirected, so the difference between `/pages` and `/pages/` would have been \"a list\" versus \"the app\". Killed a duplication on the way in: the three whole-page surfaces carried their heading, subheading and browser title as literals inside each route body, and the index needed them a fourth time. They live once in `_PAGES` now, read by both the routes (through a new `_whole_page`, the counterpart to `_setup_page`) and the index. The nine setup panels come from `_SETUP_ORDER`, so the list is in wizard order because the sequence is the meaning. A dev banner appears only when `PAGES_RELOAD_DIRS` is on, naming what is watched \u2014 the same fact `/admin/diagnostics` reports as degraded, said where somebody editing these pages is actually looking. Fragment endpoints and the reloader's event stream are left off deliberately: one returns markup with no shell, the other never finishes, and both are traps in a list you click through. The gate's real assertion is that every entry GOES somewhere, because an index is a promise about other pages and a dead link on it is worse than no index. Proved able to fail by pointing the setup links at a typo \u2014 and the useful half was NOT the status check: a dead `/pages/` link still returns 200, because the SPA shell answers, so what catches it is asserting a server-rendered page came back. First version of that assertion used `.to.contain` on the body and Chai printed the entire SPA document on failure, several screens of markup burying the one useful word; asserted as a boolean it now reads `\"/pages/admin/setup/changelog-typo?lang=en-US returned the SPA shell, not a server-rendered page\"`. Built and verified without a single rebuild \u2014 the reloader picked up a brand-new route from a live edit, which is the feature from the entry below proving itself.**\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L502`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2490
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "3ccb16571cff5796",
      "line_num": 504,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#dx"
      ],
      "text": "**The no-build pages reload themselves, and the browser follows (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 159 tests/131 passing/28 pending, nobuild 159/159 \u2014 plus `wizard_smoke` PASS and the new `make reload_gate` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Alexander, after `LIVE=1` landed: _\"I think we can go better than that... Things restart on changes to the Python files.\"_ Right, and for the third time the capability already existed and was missing from the one place a human works: `dev.sh` has run `uvicorn --reload` for years, and `manual-check.sh` boots `start.sh`, which did not. `PAGES_RELOAD_DIRS` is now the one switch for both halves \u2014 `start.sh` branches on it (a branch, not extra flags, because uvicorn refuses `--reload` with `--workers`), and the pages shell reads the same value to decide whether to serve the browser-refresh island, so the reloader and the thing that tells the browser about it cannot drift apart. The presence of a VALUE is the enable; there is no companion boolean to be set to on-while-watching-nothing. `--log-level info`, not `warning`, and that is load-bearing: the reloader announces a restart at INFO, so at `warning` it restarts in total silence, which reads as \"my edit did nothing\" \u2014 the exact failure the feature exists to remove. The browser half costs no dependency, because of one fact worth keeping: a uvicorn restart drops every open connection and `EventSource` reconnects by itself, so THE RESTART IS THE SIGNAL. Nothing watches `.py` twice \u2014 a reconnect means the server went away and came back. The first `open` is skipped, since that one is just the page loading. Assets are the opposite case and do need a watcher, because `/pages/_assets` is served from disk and a stylesheet edit restarts nothing: `watchfiles.awatch` (already present via `uvicorn[standard]`) pushes an `assets` event and the island re-stamps same-origin stylesheet hrefs without reloading, so you can restyle a panel without losing your scroll or closing the dialog you are looking at. `yield_on_timeout=True` gives the keep-alive in the same loop rather than racing `wait_for` against the watcher and cancelling it mid-poll. Measured, finally, after a whole session of saying \"~3s\": a panel edit is served 2.8 seconds after saving, with no manual restart; a mode switch is 7 seconds; a fresh boot with seeding is 8. Switching is `make review` / `review_live` / `review_rebuild`, and the friction was never typing the flag \u2014 a bind mount cannot be added to a running container, and teardown deleted the data volume, so every flip cost a re-seeded admin and a re-grafted ui-Sprig. `REUSE_DATA=1` keeps it. The gates deliberately have no such switch, said in the Makefile beside the targets that do: a guard-rail run against a working tree tests something we do not ship. Two gates, because one could not reach both states. `pages-dev-reload.cy.ts` covers the OFF state, which is what every shipped instance runs; `scripts/gates/dev-reload/run-gate.sh` (`make reload_gate`) boots its own container with the flag and edits a COPY of `pages/`, never the working tree. Three things this turned up, all of them the gates doing their job. (1) The off-state spec first asserted a 404 and went red at 200 \u2014 nothing under `/pages/` can 404, because `SPAStaticFiles` is mounted at `/` with an index.html fallback, so the shell answers. The route genuinely is not registered; the assertion now checks the observable fact (no `text/event-stream`) and says why the obvious version is wrong. (2) Proving the gate could fail by widening the reloader's include filter to `'*'` put the app in a RESTART LOOP rather than making it wrong, and the gate hung for ten minutes instead of reporting \u2014 a gate that hangs on a broken product has the same defect as one that passes on it, so every curl now carries `--max-time`. (3) `diagnostics-panel.cy.ts` went red because `Diagnostics.svelte` hardcoded its three `boot_status` keys while the no-build page loops over whatever arrives: a new check rendered on one side and silently nowhere on the other, since the `{#if}` turns a missing key into nothing rather than an error. Fixed by enumerating from the response, which kills the class rather than the instance. Proved able to fail, each break discriminating: drop `--reload` and only check 1 goes red; widen the include filter and only the no-restart check does; drop either guard and the off-state spec names it.**\n`#frontend` `#dx`\n`TODO.md:L504`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2580
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8fc736e88e7a9293",
      "line_num": 506,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**`manual-check.sh` grew `LIVE=1` \u2014 looking at a no-build page no longer costs a build (2026-07-31; Alexander: _\"Souldn't we work in such a way the no build is needed for our no build changes???\"_). Fair, and the gap was narrower than it looked: `dev_run` already bind-mounts `app/backend/`, so the capability existed \u2014 it was missing from the ONE script whose entire job is letting a human look at these surfaces. `manual-check.sh` booted the baked image with no source mount, so a one-line style tweak on a server-rendered page meant rebuilding a 619 MB image, against surfaces whose whole selling point is that there is no build step. `LIVE=1` now mounts `sage_is_ai/pages/` over the image's copy. Both halves measured rather than assumed, because they reload differently and guessing wrong reads as \"my edit did nothing\": `pages/assets/*.css` and `*.js` need only a REFRESH (the `/pages/_assets` `StaticFiles` mount reads from disk per request \u2014 proved by appending a marker and serving it with no rebuild); `pages/*.py` needs `docker restart sage-manual`, about 3 seconds, because the panel modules are imported once at boot and that start path has no `--reload` (proved 0 before the restart, 1 after). Both are printed in the walkthrough so nobody has to find out. OFF by default and that is deliberate: Phase S made the human pass a standing condition because a green suite is the weakest evidence on an interactive surface, and a review of your working tree is not a review of the artifact you ship. Default when deciding whether it ships; `LIVE=1` while still changing it. Narrower than `dev_run`'s whole-backend mount on purpose \u2014 only `pages/` is shadowed, so everything the panels call into is still baked. #frontend #dx <!-- codespell:ignore -->**\n`TODO.md:L506`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2670
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 509,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Diagnostics migrated \u2014 813 \u2192 273 lines, 67% at parity** (2026-07-28, commit `9a2bae5`). The second fragment surface, and the one that made the convention worth writing down: it more than doubled the Sprigs panel's 32% cut, and the difference is almost entirely **native HTML over custom widgets** \u2014 `<details>` replaced the Svelte `Collapsible` and deleted the 267-line how-to-fix modal outright. `fixRegistry.json` and `commandLibrary.json` are now data BOTH implementations read, so the 40 remediation steps exist once instead of being transcribed into Python. Per-row re-probe posts to a fragment route that calls `probe_endpoint` directly \u2014 that handler refuses any URL not currently configured, so reimplementing it to save lines would have routed around the SSRF defence. Detail and the parity-gate story in the two entries above."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 510,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Theme/branding migrated \u2014 288 \u2192 200 lines, 31%, with ZERO new CSS** (2026-07-29; `e2e_both` green both targets, legacy 67 tests/60 passing/7 nobuild-only pending, nobuild 67/67; uncommitted, user commits). The third fragment surface and the FIRST that is only a form, which is the shape the plan claimed htmx deletes the most code on. `/pages/admin/branding` renders the seven fields filled in, saves through `routers/configs.set_branding` directly, and swaps the whole panel back. **The measurement, after a course correction that is the real lesson here: 288 \u2192 200 non-comment lines, a 31% cut, and NOT ONE new line of CSS.** The first version of this surface hand-wrote 99 lines in `pages.css` and landed at 288 \u2192 265, an 8% cut \u2014 reported honestly as weak, with the diagnosis that the Svelte page's inline props cost zero lines while the fragment's stylesheet cost 99. Alexander read that and made the call the number was pointing at: _\"we should be using the inline props costing zero lines across the board \u2014 startr.style IS more flexible and quick to edit; don't go reinventing the wheel\"_, followed by _\"avoid custom classes when possible and avoid almost all custom elements, HTML has our back\"_. Converting to props deleted the stylesheet outright and took the cut from **8% to 31%**, in line with Sprigs' 32%. **The old rule was wrong on its own terms**: `pages.css`'s header claimed a class beats props on GENERATED markup because an f-string emitting forty characters of style per row reads worse. It does not, because the props are written ONCE in the render helper and repeated by the generator \u2014 five fields share one `_INPUT_S` string. So props cost the same source lines as a class and delete the stylesheet, which is the entire 23-line difference. That header comment is now corrected in place rather than left to mislead the next person. **Second correction, same direction**: the markup was div-and-class scaffolding and is now the elements that already mean what we needed \u2014 `<fieldset>`/`<legend>` for the field groups (grouping and accessible name, free), `<p>`/`<label>`/`<small>` for each row, `<output>` for the preview (implicit status role, so it announces after a save with no ARIA attribute written). The rendered page contains **zero class attributes and zero ARIA attributes**. The one class left in the source is the self-fading toast, because `@keyframes` is a thing the prop vocabulary genuinely cannot express \u2014 that is the test for when a class is still right. **One script was added, deliberately**: `pages/assets/color-pair.js`, 19 lines. `<input type=\"color\">` cannot express \"unset\" and unset is meaningful here (empty means \"use the theme's own colours\"), so the hex field is the authority and the island mirrors the picker to it. It is a FILE and not two `oninput` attributes because the diagnostics page tells operators to set a Content-Security-Policy, and inline handlers are the first thing a CSP stops \u2014 shipping markup that breaks when an operator follows our own advice is not a trade worth ten characters. It is also delegated from the document, so it survives htmx swapping the form out from under it. **A behaviour difference that is deliberate, not a regression**: the Svelte preview updates on every keystroke because the values are bound to a client-side model; the fragment's preview reflects SAVED branding. Reproducing live typing would mean a round-trip per keystroke or a client-side copy of the form state \u2014 the second being the duplication this migration exists to delete. Written into the spec, this entry, and the UX-review item rather than left to be discovered. **Process, in the convention's order**: registered in `surfaces.ts` first; `branding` added to the `RESTORED_CONFIG` leak guard BEFORE writing a spec that mutates it (it sets the title, logo and the `--primary` the whole UI cascades from, so a leak here repaints every later spec's page); guard-rail proven GREEN against Svelte before any code moved; then the fragment. **The parity gate passed on the first run, which is when it deserves the least trust** \u2014 the diagnostics one went red \u2014 so it was proved able to fail by deleting the save hook, rebuilding, and confirming it went red naming `branding-save` exactly. Also covered: the colour-pair island has a cross-implementation test (both implementations sync hex \u2192 picker), because an island nothing asserts is precisely how the \"Show me how to fix this\" button shipped rendering and doing nothing; only that direction is testable, since Cypress cannot open a native colour dialog, so picker \u2192 hex stays a human-pass item. `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts` gained a per-surface structural selector instead of one hardcoded to the first two surfaces, and the swatch colours travel in custom properties `pages.css` reads rather than framework props \u2014 a colour swatch is the one element here that cannot degrade to plain."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 511,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Setup wizard CUT OVER \u2014 1,816 \u2192 1,294 lines, 29%, and the modal is deleted** (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 155 tests/131 passing/24 pending, nobuild 155/155 \u2014 and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). The last Phase 2 surface and the only one that was never fragment-shaped: nine panels with real client state between them. `ChangesAndSetupModal.svelte` and its nine step components \u2014 **2,132 raw lines, 1,816 of code** \u2014 are GONE. What shows those panels to a reader now is `SetupDialog.svelte`, **168 lines**, which fetches one of the nine routes, lifts the panel out of the response and puts it in a native `<dialog>`. Nothing in it renders a panel; markup, defaults, validation and every save path live on the server, in one language, judged by the specs that already point at those routes. **A native `<dialog>` rather than `Modal.svelte`, because the element already does four things that component hand-rolls**: Escape to close, focus trapped inside, everything behind it inert, and a `::backdrop` that needs no fixed-position overlay. Those are four behaviours we no longer maintain. **Extracted, not framed.** The panel is lifted into the app's own document rather than shown in an iframe, so the nine wizard specs keep reaching their `data-cy` hooks with a plain `cy.get`, and so an operator's Content-Security-Policy has no say in whether the wizard works. **The cost of sharing a document is a stylesheet, and it was measured rather than assumed**: `app.html` already loads startr.style, and the nine panels contain **zero `class` attributes** \u2014 they are inline props end to end \u2014 so what `pages.css` still owned for them is exactly `var(--line)` and the three pager rules. Four rules scoped to the dialog cover it. Loading the sheet itself would have imported its `:root { color-scheme: light dark }` and handed every form control in the app to the OS theme instead of the app's. **How a flow ENDS is a redirect, not a flag**: `/pages/changelog/seen` and `/pages/admin/setup/complete/finish` now 303 to `/`, and the host closes when a response lands outside `/pages/`. One mechanism serves both readers \u2014 with JavaScript the dialog closes, without it the browser lands back in the app instead of on the summary it just dismissed. The route used to re-render that summary, which was a dead end nobody had noticed because the modal closed instead. **A regression this nearly shipped, caught by reading rather than by a gate**: Settings \u2192 About \u2192 \"See what's new\" is a control EVERY user has, and the old changelog panel had no role check at all. Pointing it at `/pages/admin/setup/changelog` would have turned a working button into a 403 for everyone who is not an admin. Fixed with `require_page_user` and `/pages/changelog` \u2014 the one non-admin page route, deliberately outside `/admin/`, sharing the renderer through a `base` parameter so a second copy cannot drift. **The parity gate found a real defect in this work, which is the point of it existing.** The first version left the `<dialog>` mounted and closed on every page; a closed dialog still renders its children, so `setup-dialog`, `setup-close` and `setup-content` appeared on EVERY SvelteKit page in the app, and `surface-parity` went red naming all three. Fixed by rendering it only while open, which is what the deleted modal did. **Test estate, changed deliberately rather than left to rot**: the nine wizard entries are OUT of `surfaces.ts`, because with one implementation left that gate would have compared a route against itself and passed for the wrong reason; `openLegacy` and `scope` went with them, since the wizard was their only user and an optional field nothing sets reads like a contract; `openSetupPanel()` replaces them; every `isNoBuild()` skip in the wizard specs is gone, because a guard that never skips hides the only implementation from half the runs. `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts` re-lists the nine routes explicitly \u2014 it iterated the registry, so retiring those entries would have silently dropped from that gate the nine surfaces MOST exposed to a startr.style outage. **`setup-dialog.cy.ts` is what took their place**, nine tests on the only code the cut-over added: the panel arriving, delivery losing nothing, a link and a form followed without a page load, a panel's script running, the flow ending, and the changelog reaching a non-admin. **All four new gates proved able to fail before being trusted** \u2014 extract only the panel root and the delivery gate goes red naming `setup-next, setup-prev, setup-step`; drop the script adoption and the pager gate goes red; re-render the summary instead of redirecting and the dialog is \"continuously found\"; make the changelog admin-only and the last one reports 403 where it wants 200. Escape is deliberately NOT asserted: Cypress dispatches synthetic key events a browser does not treat as real, so a test of it would pass or fail for reasons unrelated to this code \u2014 the close button, which is ours, is asserted instead. **Three filed bugs closed by deletion**: `handleWelcomeStart` skipping against a stale reactive value (the server answers now), `CompleteStep`'s unclearable 5s interval, and the orchestrator's stale panel-order comment."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 512,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#i18n",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Locale negotiation \u2014 BLOCKS any migrated page taking over a real route** (surfaced 2026-07-28 building `pages/i18n.py`). Server-rendered pages read `en-US` and nothing else, because the reader's language lives in `localStorage` where no server can see it. Today that costs nothing: `/pages/*` are additional URLs an operator opts into, and the SvelteKit page still owns the route. The moment one of them BECOMES `/admin/diagnostics`, every non-English operator silently loses their language. Needs a real decision \u2014 `Accept-Language`, a cookie written at sign-in, or a persisted user preference \u2014 and the last is probably right, since it is the only one that survives a new browser."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "90159e012dddb5d3",
      "line_num": 508,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phase 2 \u2014 ripe surfaces COMPLETE: theme/branding, wizard, diagnostics. All three migrated, and the wizard is the first surface where the SvelteKit side was actually DELETED rather than left running beside it \u2014 which is what turns the migration's line saving from a projection into a fact.**\n- [x] **Diagnostics migrated \u2014 813 \u2192 273 lines, 67% at parity** (2026-07-28, commit `9a2bae5`). The second fragment surface, and the one that made the convention worth writing down: it more than doubled the Sprigs panel's 32% cut, and the difference is almost entirely **native HTML over custom widgets** \u2014 `<details>` replaced the Svelte `Collapsible` and deleted the 267-line how-to-fix modal outright. `fixRegistry.json` and `commandLibrary.json` are now data BOTH implementations read, so the 40 remediation steps exist once instead of being transcribed into Python. Per-row re-probe posts to a fragment route that calls `probe_endpoint` directly \u2014 that handler refuses any URL not currently configured, so reimplementing it to save lines would have routed around the SSRF defence. Detail and the parity-gate story in the two entries above. #frontend\n- [x] **Theme/branding migrated \u2014 288 \u2192 200 lines, 31%, with ZERO new CSS** (2026-07-29; `e2e_both` green both targets, legacy 67 tests/60 passing/7 nobuild-only pending, nobuild 67/67; uncommitted, user commits). The third fragment surface and the FIRST that is only a form, which is the shape the plan claimed htmx deletes the most code on. `/pages/admin/branding` renders the seven fields filled in, saves through `routers/configs.set_branding` directly, and swaps the whole panel back. **The measurement, after a course correction that is the real lesson here: 288 \u2192 200 non-comment lines, a 31% cut, and NOT ONE new line of CSS.** The first version of this surface hand-wrote 99 lines in `pages.css` and landed at 288 \u2192 265, an 8% cut \u2014 reported honestly as weak, with the diagnosis that the Svelte page's inline props cost zero lines while the fragment's stylesheet cost 99. Alexander read that and made the call the number was pointing at: _\"we should be using the inline props costing zero lines across the board \u2014 startr.style IS more flexible and quick to edit; don't go reinventing the wheel\"_, followed by _\"avoid custom classes when possible and avoid almost all custom elements, HTML has our back\"_. Converting to props deleted the stylesheet outright and took the cut from **8% to 31%**, in line with Sprigs' 32%. **The old rule was wrong on its own terms**: `pages.css`'s header claimed a class beats props on GENERATED markup because an f-string emitting forty characters of style per row reads worse. It does not, because the props are written ONCE in the render helper and repeated by the generator \u2014 five fields share one `_INPUT_S` string. So props cost the same source lines as a class and delete the stylesheet, which is the entire 23-line difference. That header comment is now corrected in place rather than left to mislead the next person. **Second correction, same direction**: the markup was div-and-class scaffolding and is now the elements that already mean what we needed \u2014 `<fieldset>`/`<legend>` for the field groups (grouping and accessible name, free), `<p>`/`<label>`/`<small>` for each row, `<output>` for the preview (implicit status role, so it announces after a save with no ARIA attribute written). The rendered page contains **zero class attributes and zero ARIA attributes**. The one class left in the source is the self-fading toast, because `@keyframes` is a thing the prop vocabulary genuinely cannot express \u2014 that is the test for when a class is still right. **One script was added, deliberately**: `pages/assets/color-pair.js`, 19 lines. `<input type=\"color\">` cannot express \"unset\" and unset is meaningful here (empty means \"use the theme's own colours\"), so the hex field is the authority and the island mirrors the picker to it. It is a FILE and not two `oninput` attributes because the diagnostics page tells operators to set a Content-Security-Policy, and inline handlers are the first thing a CSP stops \u2014 shipping markup that breaks when an operator follows our own advice is not a trade worth ten characters. It is also delegated from the document, so it survives htmx swapping the form out from under it. **A behaviour difference that is deliberate, not a regression**: the Svelte preview updates on every keystroke because the values are bound to a client-side model; the fragment's preview reflects SAVED branding. Reproducing live typing would mean a round-trip per keystroke or a client-side copy of the form state \u2014 the second being the duplication this migration exists to delete. Written into the spec, this entry, and the UX-review item rather than left to be discovered. **Process, in the convention's order**: registered in `surfaces.ts` first; `branding` added to the `RESTORED_CONFIG` leak guard BEFORE writing a spec that mutates it (it sets the title, logo and the `--primary` the whole UI cascades from, so a leak here repaints every later spec's page); guard-rail proven GREEN against Svelte before any code moved; then the fragment. **The parity gate passed on the first run, which is when it deserves the least trust** \u2014 the diagnostics one went red \u2014 so it was proved able to fail by deleting the save hook, rebuilding, and confirming it went red naming `branding-save` exactly. Also covered: the colour-pair island has a cross-implementation test (both implementations sync hex \u2192 picker), because an island nothing asserts is precisely how the \"Show me how to fix this\" button shipped rendering and doing nothing; only that direction is testable, since Cypress cannot open a native colour dialog, so picker \u2192 hex stays a human-pass item. `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts` gained a per-surface structural selector instead of one hardcoded to the first two surfaces, and the swatch colours travel in custom properties `pages.css` reads rather than framework props \u2014 a colour swatch is the one element here that cannot degrade to plain. #frontend\n- [x] **Setup wizard CUT OVER \u2014 1,816 \u2192 1,294 lines, 29%, and the modal is deleted** (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 155 tests/131 passing/24 pending, nobuild 155/155 \u2014 and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). The last Phase 2 surface and the only one that was never fragment-shaped: nine panels with real client state between them. `ChangesAndSetupModal.svelte` and its nine step components \u2014 **2,132 raw lines, 1,816 of code** \u2014 are GONE. What shows those panels to a reader now is `SetupDialog.svelte`, **168 lines**, which fetches one of the nine routes, lifts the panel out of the response and puts it in a native `<dialog>`. Nothing in it renders a panel; markup, defaults, validation and every save path live on the server, in one language, judged by the specs that already point at those routes. **A native `<dialog>` rather than `Modal.svelte`, because the element already does four things that component hand-rolls**: Escape to close, focus trapped inside, everything behind it inert, and a `::backdrop` that needs no fixed-position overlay. Those are four behaviours we no longer maintain. **Extracted, not framed.** The panel is lifted into the app's own document rather than shown in an iframe, so the nine wizard specs keep reaching their `data-cy` hooks with a plain `cy.get`, and so an operator's Content-Security-Policy has no say in whether the wizard works. **The cost of sharing a document is a stylesheet, and it was measured rather than assumed**: `app.html` already loads startr.style, and the nine panels contain **zero `class` attributes** \u2014 they are inline props end to end \u2014 so what `pages.css` still owned for them is exactly `var(--line)` and the three pager rules. Four rules scoped to the dialog cover it. Loading the sheet itself would have imported its `:root { color-scheme: light dark }` and handed every form control in the app to the OS theme instead of the app's. **How a flow ENDS is a redirect, not a flag**: `/pages/changelog/seen` and `/pages/admin/setup/complete/finish` now 303 to `/`, and the host closes when a response lands outside `/pages/`. One mechanism serves both readers \u2014 with JavaScript the dialog closes, without it the browser lands back in the app instead of on the summary it just dismissed. The route used to re-render that summary, which was a dead end nobody had noticed because the modal closed instead. **A regression this nearly shipped, caught by reading rather than by a gate**: Settings \u2192 About \u2192 \"See what's new\" is a control EVERY user has, and the old changelog panel had no role check at all. Pointing it at `/pages/admin/setup/changelog` would have turned a working button into a 403 for everyone who is not an admin. Fixed with `require_page_user` and `/pages/changelog` \u2014 the one non-admin page route, deliberately outside `/admin/`, sharing the renderer through a `base` parameter so a second copy cannot drift. **The parity gate found a real defect in this work, which is the point of it existing.** The first version left the `<dialog>` mounted and closed on every page; a closed dialog still renders its children, so `setup-dialog`, `setup-close` and `setup-content` appeared on EVERY SvelteKit page in the app, and `surface-parity` went red naming all three. Fixed by rendering it only while open, which is what the deleted modal did. **Test estate, changed deliberately rather than left to rot**: the nine wizard entries are OUT of `surfaces.ts`, because with one implementation left that gate would have compared a route against itself and passed for the wrong reason; `openLegacy` and `scope` went with them, since the wizard was their only user and an optional field nothing sets reads like a contract; `openSetupPanel()` replaces them; every `isNoBuild()` skip in the wizard specs is gone, because a guard that never skips hides the only implementation from half the runs. `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts` re-lists the nine routes explicitly \u2014 it iterated the registry, so retiring those entries would have silently dropped from that gate the nine surfaces MOST exposed to a startr.style outage. **`setup-dialog.cy.ts` is what took their place**, nine tests on the only code the cut-over added: the panel arriving, delivery losing nothing, a link and a form followed without a page load, a panel's script running, the flow ending, and the changelog reaching a non-admin. **All four new gates proved able to fail before being trusted** \u2014 extract only the panel root and the delivery gate goes red naming `setup-next, setup-prev, setup-step`; drop the script adoption and the pager gate goes red; re-render the summary instead of redirecting and the dialog is \"continuously found\"; make the changelog admin-only and the last one reports 403 where it wants 200. Escape is deliberately NOT asserted: Cypress dispatches synthetic key events a browser does not treat as real, so a test of it would pass or fail for reasons unrelated to this code \u2014 the close button, which is ours, is asserted instead. **Three filed bugs closed by deletion**: `handleWelcomeStart` skipping against a stale reactive value (the server answers now), `CompleteStep`'s unclearable 5s interval, and the orchestrator's stale panel-order comment. #frontend\n- [ ] **Locale negotiation \u2014 BLOCKS any migrated page taking over a real route** (surfaced 2026-07-28 building `pages/i18n.py`). Server-rendered pages read `en-US` and nothing else, because the reader's language lives in `localStorage` where no server can see it. Today that costs nothing: `/pages/*` are additional URLs an operator opts into, and the SvelteKit page still owns the route. The moment one of them BECOMES `/admin/diagnostics`, every non-English operator silently loses their language. Needs a real decision \u2014 `Accept-Language`, a cookie written at sign-in, or a persisted user preference \u2014 and the last is probably right, since it is the only one that survives a new browser. #frontend #i18n #critical\n\n`TODO.md:L508`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2760
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "773d7321d4634458",
      "line_num": 675,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Changelog pager button does not move sides in a real browser (fixed 2026-07-31): the Continue button on `/pages/admin/setup/changelog` paged and relabelled correctly but never travelled to the other side of the row, confirmed twice by Alexander in a real browser on 2026-07-30. Fixed by deleting the mechanism rather than debugging it. The button used to travel by `transform: translateX(var(--pager-shift))`, with `changelog-pager.js` measuring `row.clientWidth - button.offsetWidth` on every sync and on every resize. It now travels by `margin-left: auto`, flipped to `margin-left: 0` by a `[data-at-end='false']` attribute the script sets \u2014 one attribute, no measurement, nothing to compute wrong. `changelog-pager.js` lost the measuring and the resize listener with it. Verified working by Alexander in a real browser 2026-07-31. Neither suspect on the old entry (a startr.style `--t` collision, a first-paint measurement race) was ever confirmed, and both describe code that no longer exists.**\n`TODO.md:L675`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2850
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "17828a5b28e059a4",
      "line_num": 685,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Setup wizard shows a step the admin explicitly unchecked (fixed 2026-07-31): deselecting Authentication on Welcome and pressing \"Get Started\" opened the Authentication panel anyway. `handleWelcomeStart` assigned `dynamicPanels` and called `skipIfNeeded()` in the same synchronous block, against a `panels` value Svelte had not recomputed \u2014 so the skip loop ran on a stale one-entry array and skipped nothing. Fixed by deletion: the orchestrator is gone, and `welcome_panel.start_wizard` now stores the choice and returns the first chosen step's route, with no reactive value to be stale. `wizard-welcome.cy.ts` asserts every step lands on its own route.**\n`TODO.md:L685`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2940
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "5e1f324ff8786ab3",
      "line_num": 687,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**`CompleteStep` leaks a 5s interval when the wizard is closed mid-download (fixed 2026-07-31): the component started a `setInterval` polling `/api/v1/retrieval/models/status` with no `onDestroy`, so closing the modal mid-download left the poll running for the rest of the session. Fixed by deletion \u2014 the panel is now `pages/complete_panel.py`, which reports what the server holds at render time and starts no timer.**\n`TODO.md:L687`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 3030
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "03c0d5cb214dc6c3",
      "line_num": 689,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**`UsersStep` offers a role its own CSV importer rejects (fixed 2026-07-30): both the Svelte importer and `pages/users_panel.CSV_ROLES` now accept `facilitator`. `pending` was kept, because removing it would break CSV files that import correctly today. Asserted in `wizard-users.cy.ts`.**\n`TODO.md:L689`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 3120
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "bb2731b03a14d813",
      "line_num": 691,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**`ChangesAndSetupModal` comment states a stale panel order (fixed 2026-07-31): the file is deleted. The order it described now lives once, in `_SETUP_ORDER` in `pages/router.py`, and `setup-navigation.cy.ts` discovers it by walking `setup-next` rather than restating it.**\n`TODO.md:L691`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 3210
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 733,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Decide where `TRY_SAGE_LLM_API_KEY` lives in production: plain env, Docker secret mount, or external vault. Recommend Docker secret for try.sage.is itself, plain env for self-hosted workshops."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 734,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "note:As we use cap rover and the system injects env vars we're leaning this way"
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f2bb0b7854af3d94",
      "line_num": 732,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**try.sage Production Decisions: (Alexander Somma + Izzy Plante) \u2014 Surfaced by Docker exploration. Block CapRover one-click rollout.**\n- [x] Decide where `TRY_SAGE_LLM_API_KEY` lives in production: plain env, Docker secret mount, or external vault. Recommend Docker secret for try.sage.is itself, plain env for self-hosted workshops.\n- [x] note:As we use cap rover and the system injects env vars we're leaning this way\n\n`TODO.md:L732`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 3300
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "b907aaa747498e2a",
      "line_num": 737,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#tests"
      ],
      "text": "**Cypress E2E revival \u2014 docker-only headless + interactive-watch infra, three new specs (2026-07-02 to 2026-07-03): Pinned `cypress/included:15.18.0` (not the 13.x devDep major \u2014 Cypress 13 bundles a Chromium whose `ReadableStream` isn't async-iterable and the app's streaming path throws on it). `scripts/e2e/run-cypress.sh` runs the suite headless against a fresh rootstock + Caddy TLS sidecar, no npm/cypress on the host. `scripts/e2e/run-cypress-watch.sh` + `scripts/e2e/watch/` adds an interactive GUI variant: Xvfb + x11vnc + noVNC served at `localhost:6080/vnc.html`, Sage-branded (logo, favicons, Archivo type via a subsetted font \u2014 see the font-subsetting TODO above). New specs: `degradation.cy.ts`, `sprigs-panel.cy.ts`, `users.cy.ts`; `registration.cy.ts` moved to `cypress/e2e/upstream/`. Headless run verified green: 10/10 specs passed. Decided 2026-07-02 alongside the noVNC-transport backlog item below (WebRTC swap still pending).**\n`#tests`\n`TODO.md:L737`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 3390
    }
  ]
}